insert into public.chunks (id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length)
select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "85812a97-b6f2-462d-90f6-7dd88f6d7b35", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (09:57): I think a lot of it has to do with the way that we interact with each other. It's constantly changing. So when you look at the development of Instagram, it started very much as this platform to post images with inspirational captions. Brands often would use quotes and lots of hashtags. The feed was chronological and it has morphed into more of a storytelling platform where you now have reels and you have video and there was IGTV for a while and now there's still a way to post longer form video content, but you're starting to see the way that people interact with each other change. And so I think that that's a big catalyst for whether a new platform will stick or not. Are you meeting your current audience where they want to interact with your brand?\n\nCole Heilborn (10:51): Interesting. So I got to imagine if I'm a social media app developer, I could develop something that is either behind the times or too far ahead of where society is at and it's maybe finding that sweet spot that kind of pushes people's imagination and opportunity without going too far or creating something that's kind of a dinosaur.\n\nKaim York-Feirn (11:16): Yeah, it's funny that you say that about a mix of different concepts because I feel like that's TikTok Vine is another great example of a social media platform that popped up and then kind of died. I don't truly understand why that faded away or why brands never jumped on that, but TikTok has replaced Vine in that sense of creating short form video. And when TikTok first launched, I think the longest that you could post was a 15 second video and now has evolved into up to three minutes worth of content. And so that really speaks to the rise of video and people wanting to watch longer form content and actually sticking around and watching the full thing if it is relatable.", "token_estimate": 463, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "09:57", "end_timestamp": "11:16", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "c996516a-e216-4c38-8dab-d208736bc967", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (12:08): Yeah, interesting. So jumping back to what you mentioned earlier, the importance of a brand doing its due diligence to understand how it's going to be represented on a platform, the first thing that I think you need to have, and it sounds like you need to have, is understanding your brand first and foremost. And from that you start to understand who we are, who we aren't. I think a lot of people understand that concept. That's something we've talked about on the show quite a bit, and there's obviously some pretty amazing brands out there that we could all look at and say, yeah, we know who they are and what their voice is. But getting more to your level where how do you take, let's say a brand book and if you're brought in to start to try to understand how are we talking on these platforms? How do you start?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (12:55): That's a great question, Cole. And to me, there are four main ingredients. The first is defining your brand mission next, your vision, and finally your values, and then finally looking at your audience demographics. So as long as your business has a mission, vision and values from that, you can glean those adjectives that I was referencing earlier and start to define who your brand is, how you want to be perceived by your customers. And then that final piece is looking at where your audience engages most online and meeting them in that space.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:37): Okay. So can we use Rough Wear as an example? How do you show up on a place like Instagram and how did you get to that conclusion?", "token_estimate": 393, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kaim York-Feirn"], "start_timestamp": "12:08", "end_timestamp": "13:37", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "51ba27ca-5b31-4429-aa01-74dc02288b6c", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (13:48): Sure. I think one key thing to point out before I talk a little bit about rough wear is that it's okay for your brand to have a different voice on different platforms, and I'd love to dig into that a little bit more later. But when it comes to Instagram, I would say that rough wear is very adventurous and playful and forward thinking, but we also create very high quality products. And so we want to display that to our customers, and that comes across in our captions, in the photos that we choose, in the way that we interact in dms. So for example, we are a brand that is okay using emojis in our dms and we are starting to use them a little bit more in our captions. And that's something that's a bit newer strategy, but when you look at our audience demographics, we do tend to relate to a little bit younger audience that is also pretty equally split between male and female, but emojis are huge when you're looking at Gen Z, millennials, et cetera. And so that's a great way to come off as relatable and as somebody who is also knowledgeable, but meeting that customer in their particular space,\n\nCole Heilborn (15:06): It's wild to think that, I mean, I get it, but it's wild to think that you can spend that much time trying to decide should I use an emoji smiley face and how will that connect or not connect with who we're talking to?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (15:21): Yeah, and one of the adjectives I would also use to describe us is friendly. And so while we're not necessarily seeking to be your friend per se, we want to relate to people in a way that they would talk to their friends. So we'll use emojis in our responses. We will try to be a little bit less formal and more personal. I think that sometimes there are customer service teams that use very copy and paste responses to questions, and I believe there is a time and a place for that. But when we're talking about Instagram and Instagram dms, that's a place where you can be a lot more personable and treat that person more like a one-on-one conversation.", "token_estimate": 513, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:48", "end_timestamp": "15:21", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "238f4bcd-f4d3-4de7-a195-587e7cc6a0b7", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:06): Sure. So how did you arrive at those adjectives? If we were to back up? How do those adjectives on Instagram support rough wear's overall brand?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (16:17): So thinking back to our adjectives, rough Wear is a very adventurous and playful brand because when you think about dogs, you think about those aspects. So they're very dury and goofy and playful, and they are your best friend. They go everywhere with you. You want to take them on lots of adventures. They are your adventure buddy. And so that was pretty easy for us to come up with those adjectives when thinking about Instagram, because we not only want to be relatable, but we also want to show that the outdoors are a space for every person, every dog, every type of adventure\n\nCole Heilborn (16:55): Sidebar. But do you guys get flooded with user generated content of people and their dogs?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (17:02): Yes. Our dms are full of people tagging us in stories, people sending us pictures. It's very common as a dog owner to be proud and think your dog is the best dog. They're all good boys and good girls. And so that's one of my favorite jobs or favorite parts about this job is people submitting photos of their dogs doing different things, seeing them go skiing together and go hiking, and maybe it's a puppy going swimming for the first time, or a dog who's sticking its tongue out and just looks super derpy. We get tons of different photos and UGC content submitted.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:42): As a fellow dog owner, I think my wife and I, yeah, like most dog owners think our dog is the best and is the weirdest, cutest dog out there and no other dog compares. But yeah, I don't know if that trend has become more popular, but I feel like in the last, I dunno, five or 10 years, maybe that's become more of a thing, maybe not. Maybe that's just Instagram has become more of a thing in that time, so we see more of it.", "token_estimate": 484, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kaim York-Feirn"], "start_timestamp": "16:06", "end_timestamp": "17:42", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "83f0041a-a7e6-4f0f-8465-5659edb0ba58", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (18:11): I will 100% agree, Bella, my Bernice Mountain dog is the cutest, dirtiest, best girl, best dog on the planet best. So anytime people want to have a play date, I'm always down. But I think Instagram, like you're saying, is that place where we express that and we share our joy of adventuring with our dogs.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:34): Yeah. So you mentioned the idea that a brand can have a different flavor of a voice on different platforms. How does that break down for a rough wear?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (18:44): Sure. So I want to talk about the platforms in general, and I can use a rough wear example when we're talking about it, but Instagram is a much more video oriented platform. It's very story forward, more aspirational. So I would say that rough wear, we'll post more of our video content there. We're looking at doing some more reels and creating user generated content. This is where we really want to relate and share our customer's stories, our customer's journeys, our employees stories. And so I think that when you're talking about social media, you also can't really ignore the conversation about sales. I will say that rough wear is less focused on social media as a sales platform, which I love. And it's much more of an educational platform where you can come to learn about the brand and start your consumer purchase journey. But for us, it's a place where Instagram is the place where we want you to come and be able to see yourself in our content.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:52): Gotcha.", "token_estimate": 381, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:11", "end_timestamp": "19:52", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "13c18404-0790-4f2e-a7ca-741c4a9d9968", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (19:53): I guess moving on to Facebook, I would say that that's more of an informational and educational platform. You'll often see brands creating private Facebook groups when you're looking at their updates, they're about products or new blog posts. So thinking about the way that I interact with Facebook as a consumer, I'm looking to learn about a brand's contact information or their hours or look at their product offering. Did they launch a new product yet? I'm in a few private Facebook groups where brands will share exclusive deals and do giveaways and offer discounts. So more educational, less personal and storytelling forward. I'll say that TikTok as one of the newer platforms is a place where we go to watch relatable content. They use popular sounds, music. I think it's way more funny. There's a lot of one-liners. You'll often hear sounds that shoot from the hip. I think that it's a place where brands can let loose a little bit, be a little bit less serious. And then Twitter sadly I think is about to go away RIP, but that's the place where you would share some of your shorter updates, maybe an opinion or two.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:14): Interesting. Do you think that's on the decline?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (21:17): I do. I have seen over the last three years that decline in the usage of Twitter, especially in brand strategy. Rough wear has a Twitter account, but I don't think it's been used since 2016 or so. So going back to that concept of meeting your customers where they are and evaluating whether a platform is right for your brand strategy, I think Twitter is a perfect example of that. It's not going to fit into everybody's strategy, especially as we move and evolve into video being the king or queen of content.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:56): It's funny, I completely forgot that Twitter was a thing. I've never understood it. That's not saying a whole lot, but I always think of it as a platform where the president posts stuff.", "token_estimate": 493, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:53", "end_timestamp": "21:56", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "0c6f66df-4ddb-48e5-9578-5c8015330d7c", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (22:10): Well, it's also kind of like Google Plus. I will hear about Google Plus in some marketing webinar and I'm like, oh my gosh, that did used to be a thing. And when I worked at an agency, we very much had a Google Plus strategy, but that died years ago. And I think Twitter has the same fate.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:34): Is a place like LinkedIn something that a brand like Rough Wear considers or how does that fit into the equation?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (22:42): Yes, I'm glad you brought that up. LinkedIn, in the last couple years, I've seen evolve from being more of a personal networking tool to much more of a brand networking tool. And so as Rough Wear's strategy goes, we do post on LinkedIn and we will share some of our blog posts, definitely our job openings and career opportunities and the occasional internal announcement or things that that we're excited about. And I have definitely started to engage more personally with LinkedIn in the last year or so. I think it's a great place to go and read news articles. Ironically, that's where I learned about a lot that's going on in the marketing world or the stocks. That's where I learned about the avocado stuff. So I definitely do think that brands have a place on LinkedIn.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:42): I think it's become one of my favorite places to go. I think it's the only place that I actively engage or try to my best to keep up with posting something.", "token_estimate": 359, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:10", "end_timestamp": "23:42", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "ee0b0969-19b7-43b8-83c9-8b4942b7834c", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (23:53): I definitely think it's a platform where you can go to get different opinions on things. So I know that one of the big topics right now in marketing is whether to pay ambassadors or if Gear Exchange is still an acceptable way of doing those programs. And so I was reading an article this morning and there were close to 50 comments on it, and I'll say, I didn't know about 80% of the people commenting, but it's really good and interesting to glean information from people's different perspectives. And so you never know who's going to see your posts on LinkedIn. It's not like Facebook where it only gets shown to the people that are following you when you post on LinkedIn. It kind of just goes into more of the vast network. And so again, yeah, I think it's a great place to look and get different perspectives on a particular topic or story.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:52): So correct me if I'm wrong, but Facebook used to have that sort of an algorithm correct, that LinkedIn has now, and I feel like that's one of the reasons why folks love LinkedIn is because it is let's say more organic. Do you feel like LinkedIn realizes that and they'll hang on to that algorithm or will they go the more Facebook route in the future?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (25:14): I certainly hope they hang on to the good thing they have going for them right now. The interesting thing about Facebook, I'll agree with what you said, and I think it really changed for them when they went to the pay to play model and you started seeing a lot more brands doing paid advertising and Facebook boosted the types of ads that they were offering. So you went from a single image ad to being able to now do a carousel ad and video ads. And then a couple years ago they introduced Canvas, which I believe is now called the Instant Experience, where you click through what looks like a single image ad, but then it takes you to a landing page, which you can look exactly like your website, which is more experience oriented. So yeah, I'll say that Facebook now is much more of an experience for the customer, whereas LinkedIn is still the more traditional news, chronological traditional way to get your news.", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:53", "end_timestamp": "25:14", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "44d275ef-be7f-4f2f-9907-08a925888e04", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (26:21): I mean, all these platforms are constantly changing and evolving. Does that make it tough as a marketer if you're like, okay, LinkedIn today is X and Facebook is X. Well, in a week from now they could be totally different. And if your voice is kind of following the platform and the audience and it's this equation, how do you manage the platforms changing?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (26:43): Oh my gosh, I am laughing so hard right now. It's a challenge. I don't envy anybody else who's in my position who has a social media manager title. We are constantly subscribed to different newsletters and we're reading about how the algorithms change week to week. Instagram is about to go back to chronological, which I think is a really interesting update for 'em. They also are about to introduce a new style of feed where you can click different buttons to see the people you follow versus explore and other things. So it is constantly evolving. We are constantly reading articles and trying to keep up on what's new. But going back to how we started this conversation, I think that's why it's really important to know who your brand is so that you can evaluate whether a platform is still working for you or if there is a shiny new object, be able to evaluate it with logic and say, yes, this is something that we want to pursue, or this is something we want to keep an eye on and let some other people try it first. I think it's completely okay for a brand to be a late adopter. You don't have to jump on these trends every single time.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:08): So I feel like that idea goes against maybe the advice that is out there all heard like, oh man, if only you were on the ground floor with Instagram, you could, I don't know, have billions of followers. That seems to be a great motivation to try to jump on something new. But you're suggesting waiting and a pause is okay.", "token_estimate": 475, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kaim York-Feirn"], "start_timestamp": "26:21", "end_timestamp": "28:08", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "8fa8232c-ba96-42fc-95ee-54d825111eaa", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (28:32): I definitely think a pause is okay. And a possible good example of this is the blue check mark that Instagram has added to what they call verified profiles. So when that feature was first released, it was released to help consumers identify whether a brand was real or if it was a fake account. But as we evolve and time goes on and we all continue posting on these platforms, it has become much less important. I think that Blue check mark was kind of that shiny object for a while where it was like, oh yeah, I want that. How do I get that? It's elusive. We are this real brand, but we can't seem to get this blue check mark. And I remember in my time at Osprey, I spent years trying to get a blue check mark and then was finally able to get it through working with our PR agency, and it seemed like it was almost too easy or too good to be true the way that that happened. (29:38): So now as a consumer, when I look at something like that, I'm like, well, what is the true value of that check mark? Do brands really need to have that? Do consumers really care about that? And so to bring that back to this concept of evaluating whether a platform is good for you or not, I think it's okay to watch and see how an idea evolves because maybe it's not so important to have that blue check mark. Maybe you don't need to be on TikTok. Maybe you're not ready to be on TikTok. And one thing that I have definitely seen is brands who will jump on a platform or jump on a trend too early where they're not ready and they create content and they get hyped about it and they do it for a while, but then you see it die off and you're like, oh, well, what happened to X brand on TikTok? They haven't released a new video in months, and I was really excited about following them. And so then all of a sudden that excitement for the consumer dies out and we move on to the next thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:38): It sounds like playing the long game and maybe being more consistent is more important than jumping on something new.", "token_estimate": 510, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "28:32", "end_timestamp": "30:38", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "dcfc83de-d708-49cf-b694-d1405a47e4e4", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (30:47): I would say for brands, consistency is key. I would rather as a social media marketer, be consistent and focus my time on one or two platforms, then try to be on five and have a very inconsistent strategy. So I guess to give you an example earlier, I think podcasting actually is a perfect example. So while it might not necessarily be a brand, I think an individual can also count or where you'll see a show pop up and they give you five or six episodes and then they say, okay, we'll be back for season two on X day. And that day comes and that show isn't back. So I'm a really big True Crime podcast listener, and that happens quite often. There's a show called Up and Vanished that has gone through three seasons and three different iterations. There's a show called Helen Gone, which also is one of those where they got halfway through a season and then stopped posting episodes for a while, and I stopped listening and then all of a sudden six months later, they were like, oh, hey, we're back. But by that time as a consumer, I had lost interest in that story. And so now I'm not going to go all the way back to the beginning and listen and get caught up again, only to in my mind, be disappointed again.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:16): Yeah, that's a great point. So let's say a brand in this example, this podcast starts off strong, it's awesome, it attracts a bunch of attention and then they don't follow through. And is that a positive or a negative long-term towards the brand or the person that's making the show? Do you come out ahead or behind at the end of the day in an example like that?", "token_estimate": 406, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:47", "end_timestamp": "32:16", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "879b261e-c5f1-496f-b39f-3144591af4b7", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (32:44): Yeah, that's so tough because my brain wants to say behind, but I don't want to feel like I have that negative outlook on things. But when you look at consumer behavior currently, we have a very short attention span. We have all of these distractions, whether that's podcasts and new music, Netflix shows, Facebook ads, Instagram, TikTok. So me as a consumer has to decide where I'm going to spend my time, what platform is most important to me, and I like consistency. I think a lot of consumers also appreciate consistency and they're going to seek out the platforms that fulfill that need for them. So when a brand or an individual posts 10 episodes of a podcast and then that podcast just dies, they're naturally going to move on to the next thing. So we might make it through all 10 episodes, but then in their mind, oh, it's over. (33:47): There's nothing else to listen to. What new podcast can I listen to? And I think about my personal behavior. I love binging a podcast. I think it's challenging for me personally to subscribe to something that comes out every week unless there's a repository of episodes that I can go back and listen to. If I love a show, I want to listen to it all the time when I'm taking my dog for a walk, when I'm driving to the coffee shop, if I'm at the airport. And so in your example, I personally would probably unsubscribe to that particular brand and move on to something that fits my needs a little bit better.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:32): Interesting. Yeah, so I mean, I can see how that gets translated across various platforms with various initiatives.\n\nKaim York-Feirn (34:39): When you're talking about consistency, I think that that applies everywhere. If you're not posting consistently on Instagram, you're not going to show up in the algorithm. If you're not posting consistently on TikTok, again, your videos aren't going to show up on the explore page or I'm sorry for you page or an algorithm. I think that also goes for your hashtag strategy. So yeah, you're totally right in that this idea of consistency to me is probably the most important thing when you're talking about a brand strategy.", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "32:44", "end_timestamp": "34:39", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "5509f5f1-1bac-4013-91cd-c6911bef15ec", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:12): So thinking about the cadence of consistency, I know there's the whole algorithm thing, but I guess I kind of want to ignore that is always changing, but you're talking about consistency. Are you saying you need to be posting once a day, once a week? What if a brand only posts once a month, but they're consistent with that? How does that work\n\nKaim York-Feirn (35:33): When you're talking about Instagram? Once a month is going to be a challenging strategy. Again, I think you have to go back and look at some of the metrics for success that have been set for the individual platforms. Unfortunately, Instagram is one of those where you need to be engaging every week. I think if you're posting two or three times a week, that's great. If you're posting once a week even, I think that that can be an okay strategy for Instagram. But when you're looking at TikTok, I believe that that almost needs to be a daily or every other day strategy. And when you look at some of the bigger brands that are on there like the NFL or some of the news channels or some of the influencers, they are definitely creating new content almost every day. And TikTok specifically is very timely. So when you have a trending sound, you need to use that immediately rather than waiting a couple months because it's not technically trending anymore, and you're not going to get as much traffic as you would if you had jumped on that right away.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:43): Okay. I don't understand TikTok, what do you mean by a sound?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (36:49): So the way that TikTok works is you can upload videos and then put a sound over them. So that can either be someone talking, maybe it's like a movie quote that somebody has uploaded or a part of a song that's been uploaded from Spotify or another music platform, or also something that's pretty popular are Siri sounds. So Siri speaking back something that you've typed. And so the great thing about TikTok is you can add a sound to your favorites and then go back and use that later in your own videos. But that's what I mean by sounds.", "token_estimate": 524, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kaim York-Feirn"], "start_timestamp": "35:12", "end_timestamp": "36:49", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "cd832903-ce33-43e6-ba39-9c13144b2dc6", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (37:31): Got it. So if I'm a brand, and I'll use myself here as an example in TikTok, I look at that and I've never really taken the time to try to understand it or really do anything with it. And I have this cynical perspective of it. If a brand, let's say something like TikTok pops up or the next big thing, and you kind have this like, oh, that seems like it's for teenagers or whatever, is that the wrong attitude to have if I'm a brand?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (38:03): Not necessarily. So again, to go back to how we started this conversation, I think it's important to look at what your strategy is and does that fit into your goals for your business? So TikTok is a great way to meet Gen Z where they're at and some of the younger demographics, but we are also starting to see millennials or what they're calling elder millennials. There's a trending funny sound going around that's like if you are really old, if you were born in the nineties and there's just people reacting and their hearts just sink and they're like, oh my gosh, I'm old to be on TikTok, and that would totally be true for me. I was born in 88, so I'm in my thirties. And so to be called Ancient on TikTok by the younger generation, you just kind of have to laugh about it. But no, I don't think that that's a cynical way of looking at it necessarily. If it doesn't fit into your business objectives, I actually think that that's a rather smart way of looking at it because maybe your time is better suited, for instance, doing podcasting and posting these to LinkedIn and other places where you're going to get that listenership.", "token_estimate": 405, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kaim York-Feirn"], "start_timestamp": "37:31", "end_timestamp": "38:03", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "82bb5cbd-68e9-4bec-b1fd-76f7cf95db87", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (39:22): Gotcha. Okay. So thinking back to our conversation that we had before this conversation, we talked about some of the work you had done at Osprey, and during your time at Osprey, you guys had created a Spotify playlist that was promoted or it was Osprey branded. That seems like such a cool thing. I know that's a really, I see that at Starbucks or coffee shops. You can take Starbucks home with you, take the sounds of Starbucks home or whatever. And actually just this morning I was listening to a playlist that Yeti puts out every week or every month maybe. What's, what's up with that? It seems really cool. I really like the music that Yeti has on their playlist, and I'm like, I like this stuff. I can get down with it. But what was the goal for you guys at Osprey while you did that?", "token_estimate": 203, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "39:22", "end_timestamp": "39:22", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "f42aefc8-11bf-4a57-a06e-0efd89aac137", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (40:18): It's so funny that you bring up Yeti because they were a bit of the inspiration behind starting that channel. So one thing that was very unique to Osprey I think, was that we worked with a lot of bands, and I didn't realize that when I first started there, but we supported a lot of artists and different bands through partnerships and would send them gear and get to go to concerts, and they would come to our office sometimes and do shows for our employees. And I wanted a way to be able to share that with our audience. And so we started with some Instagram live events and those were fun, but then I thought, not everybody is going to tune into an Instagram live. How can we allow them to take this home and listen whenever they want? And again, going back to this concept of meet your audience where they interact, music is a great way for people to connect to each other regardless of what your profession is, how old you are, anything like that. (41:25): Music is a huge part of our everyday lives. And so that's where the idea was born is if we can create these Spotify playlists, we're not only meeting our customers where they interact with us, we are giving them an inside look into who we are as a brand by creating these playlists by different bands that we work with by different influencers that we work with. And so it kind of sparked a whole idea of, okay, if an influencer writes us a blog, maybe they can also create a playlist that is inspired by their adventure or their trip. It's a way to introduce people to a new side of our brand. Again, it was kind of a fun platform to be on. It fit very well with our vision and values, and very quickly, I think we gained close to 800 followers within the first month of launching it. And so I think you're the perfect example of why it works. In your question, you brought up, oh, take Starbucks home with you and that you found Yeti's playlist. So again, I think that that just kind of validates why a strategy that works", "token_estimate": 503, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn"], "start_timestamp": "40:18", "end_timestamp": "40:18", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "e7f57a4b-1d69-4add-bf8b-82209493a2a6", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Cole Heilborn (42:43): In the example of Yeti, it seems like it's such a, dunno what the right word is, but it's the least in your face. I feel like marketing or social media because you're consuming something that isn't theirs. You're consuming an artist's song, but it's been curated and crafted by this brand and then present it to you as, I don't know how you decide what goes on that thing, but I'm sure it connects to their brand. And it sounds very Yeti, if I could say that.\n\nKaim York-Feirn (43:20): I think it's very on brand for Yeti. I think it's very on brand for a lot of outdoor brands in general. It's something that we are also starting to work into our strategy here at Rough Wear. When you think about adventure, I definitely think about music. I am constantly listening to a new artist or a podcast whenever I'm driving. And so giving people new artists to listen to, giving people a new podcast and inspiration, I think is a great way to connect with them. Like you're saying that's not super in your face. I think there are times when marketing kind of gets a bad rap for being a little bit too showy or promotional, and Spotify is one of those places that seems a lot more organic.\n\nCole Heilborn (44:12): Yeah, well, even as you're just talking about it, I think everyone has their ski mountain bike pump up playlist or your workout playlist. And I think a lot of people craft those personally. But what if a ski brand had the ultimate ski pump up playlist and you could turn that on every time you're driving up to the mountain", "token_estimate": 388, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kaim York-Feirn"], "start_timestamp": "42:43", "end_timestamp": "44:12", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "78721e96-404d-40d6-8a0f-751668777926", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (44:35): When you touch on something That's kind of interesting too. We as consumers trust individuals and influencers more than we trust a brand. So that was again, another inspiration behind this is if a ski brand can get a very famous skier to create this playlist, people are going to be pumped about that. Like, oh, I'm listening to Michaela Schifrin's skiing playlist that she listens to before she races. How cool is that? Or I'm listening to this playlist curated by this band with songs that influence their music. So it's not only a great way for the consumer to discover new music, it's a great marketing tool that again, is not in your face and seems very relatable.\n\nCole Heilborn (45:27): So when you started the playlist at Osprey, was there any sort of ROI that you were looking for or how did you decide if this was worth pursuing?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (45:37): That's a great question. ROI, no, I don't think that in our minds Spotify was going to be a revenue generator. The biggest goal for us was to grow our following on a different platform, so increase our audience size and gain new followers or customers in a different space. So for example, or just to clarify, somebody that follows you on Facebook doesn't necessarily follow you on Instagram. Someone that follows you on TikTok also doesn't necessarily follow you on Instagram. So you want to be in as many places as possible or as makes sense for your brand in order to reach the most amount of people.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:27): So the Spotify playlist, the idea was hopefully to pick up people that maybe don't follow the other accounts and in doing so, maybe they go follow the Osprey Instagram.\n\nKaim York-Feirn (46:39): Right, exactly.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:41): Interesting. Okay, so I've got another question for you, and I know you're very passionate about this topic. You alluded to it earlier, and I definitely want to circle back the idea of sales on social media. Give me your gut reaction to this", "token_estimate": 499, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "44:35", "end_timestamp": "46:41", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "47cc07be-9ac4-4e99-a14b-500bb81ddbde", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (46:58): Gut reaction is that social media is not a sales tool. You're right. I am very passionate about this topic, and I think it's an interesting conversation because it's very different for different brands. So in my mind and in my roles that I've had in marketing, I believe social media to be a tool where you relate to your customers, where you can educate your customers and you help them along the buying journey. But one thing that we didn't really touch on is I have a journalism background, and so I am very much a storyteller. The way that I got into social media was because I thought I wanted to be a magazine writer. I graduated during the recession when print advertising and media was starting to decline. And so social media seemed like a natural way for me to still be a storyteller while also growing my career and learning new things about marketing. So that's my stance on social media is I think it is very much a storytelling and educational platform.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:11): So when Instagram rolled out their buy now feature or you could tag product a few years ago first, is that still a thing's still\n\nKaim York-Feirn (48:21): Definitely still a thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (48:23): Okay. So how does that change the platform or how does that change the experience that the customer has or not the customer, the audience? Well, I guess now they're both, right. They're the same thing,", "token_estimate": 356, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "46:58", "end_timestamp": "48:23", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "3c3e26a9-0b3a-49d0-b146-45b55f17e013", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (48:37): And my response is twofold. So for a lot of brands, I think it can make sense. It depends on what you're selling and how your audience interacts with your brand or how you've trained them to interact with your brand. So for me personally, I have never purchased a product off of Instagram or Facebook, nor do I see myself ever doing that. I trust a website much more than I trust a social media platform, but then that's kind of a double-edged sword because this is my job, so I kind of have to promote this and work on a strategy that mixes this into what we post. So in my mind, when I'm tagging a product on Instagram, it's more for awareness. If somebody sees an image that has our frontline harness on it, a front range harness on it or a specific leash, I want them to be able to tap that image and say, oh yeah, I want to go check that out and learn more about that. And then they're ultimately going to go to our website and decide if they want to convert or not. But what I'm hoping is that that social media post was more of an educational piece that pushed them to make a purchase down the line.\n\nCole Heilborn (50:02): Gotcha. Why do you trust a website for a purchase over a brand social media page?", "token_estimate": 310, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "48:37", "end_timestamp": "50:02", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "0bd70b59-4742-4808-8b8c-95261f7913a0", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (50:11): Well, for one, their websites have been around for a long time, and you have platforms like Shopify. Gosh, I'm not super into e-comm, so I know that there are others, but there very trusted sources that manage all of the money and the backend of that and inventory. And I have not had many bad experiences with the purchasing side of a product from a website, whereas selling on social media is a fairly new concept. I'll say that it's come out in the last five to 10 years. So in my consumer brain, it's still fairly new, and I think that there are a lot of kinks to work out. I remember being invited to one of the first iterations of Instagram shopping when I worked at Osprey, and we spent a fair amount of time on the phone with their sales team vetting whether that was something that fit into our strategy or not. And ultimately at the time it did not. (51:20): And so yeah, I think it just kind of goes back to the longevity of it and seeing success with website stuff. I know that it for sure works. I manage some of those metrics and I see that people do purchase from that. The other side of that is that those platforms are very influential with a purchase. So when you see a TikTok person wearing a specific pair of leggings or an outfit, they'll often talk about where they bought it from. Sometimes it's paid for, it's an advertisement, but if I'm scrolling through my feed and I'm like, oh, that's cute, then I'm going to go to the website and follow up.\n\nCole Heilborn (52:03): Yeah, there's something about having a differentiation between where you're consuming and learning and having fun versus where you're making a decision to purchase something like having a line in the sand or two different Instagram versus an Amazon, it seems more comforting or it seems more like you want to keep those things separated almost.\n\nKaim York-Feirn (52:31): Yeah, I would agree with that. I like to keep those two concepts very separate.", "token_estimate": 493, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "50:11", "end_timestamp": "52:31", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "5ddbb6cc-1944-49db-8fc7-a9a98826a61a", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Cole Heilborn (52:38): So Kami, you've quickly now become, I think my contacts for all things social because I see something and I'm like, oh, I wonder what this means, or, I don't know anything about this. But now that I know that you exist, I might be hitting you up with some questions. So I've got one more. I feel like we can't talk about social media without talking about the Metaverse.\n\nKaim York-Feirn (53:01): Okay, let's do it.\n\nCole Heilborn (53:02): So again, I don't really know much about it, but I'm curious just, I mean, I watched Facebook's or Meta's 10 minute introduction video, but what's your reaction?", "token_estimate": 156, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kaim York-Feirn"], "start_timestamp": "52:38", "end_timestamp": "53:02", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "eb797938-b6fa-4039-8780-418d7a327d6c", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "Kaim York-Feirn (53:17): I think initial gut reaction is Facebook is trying to take over the world. I don't know if that's a good thing or a bad thing. Honestly, I think it was pretty inevitable. When you look at their track record, they purchased Instagram a while back. I also believe they purchased WhatsApp. And so they're buying up platforms that make sense to build this metaverse and try and sort of become a Google of the social media universe. For better or for worse, I mean, Facebook is the giant, when you look at social media strategy, you can't get away with spending money on the internet without giving some of it to Facebook. And so I think they want to continue to be this high power where people come for information and to sell their products. I mean, at the end of the day, meta is going to do what Meta is going to do, and I think we're all just pieces of the puzzle and we have to decide how invested we are in their growth and continue to watch what they do and whether that continues to align with our brand values and whether we want to continue spending money on their platform. (54:39): And I'd say for now, absolutely it makes sense for a lot of brands. I know there were brands during the pandemic like Patagonia that decided to completely pull their advertising budget from Facebook. They can do that because they are a giant when it comes to the outdoor industry, and they get revenue from places that maybe some smaller brands don't. In a sense, I think that that's a little bit unfair that some smaller brands can't get off of Facebook, they just financially cannot afford to because it's a place where they generate a lot of revenue, whereas some of the larger brands can't afford to pull part of their spend or their full spend. So they have created this pay to play environment where it's here to stay, it's not going anywhere. And again, we're all just kind of little pieces in their puzzle.\n\nCole Heilborn (55:40): Interesting. And I guess we just got to wait and see what happens.\n\nKaim York-Feirn (55:43): Wait and see what happens. Facebook is going to take over the world someday whether we like it or not.", "token_estimate": 538, "speakers": ["Kaim York-Feirn", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "53:17", "end_timestamp": "55:43", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "1158192f-1bcd-46b4-b9f1-1818886061a2", "episode_id": "8d8ebe7a-5cd3-43e7-ad82-76ba53105198", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488c", "chunk_index": 28, "content": "Cole Heilborn (55:51): Yep. Well, I think it's interesting. We started this conversation discussing brand voice and how that translates onto social media, and I think is evident in this conversation. Social media is a huge topic, but because it's so huge, I think that even means you have to be more focused and have more clarity around how you're working with it and how you are anticipating things like the Metaverse and how do you respond to that. Yeah. Kami, I want to thank you for your time today. I think this has been a really insightful conversation about a lot of different things, but if nothing else, it sounds like just hang on to those core values, mission and understanding who your audience is. Is that right?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (56:34): Yeah. And thank you so much for this conversation, Cole. I've appreciated a lot of your questions, and I think I will just end by saying that defining your brand voice is a collaborative effort. It's certainly not just done by social media managers. I think a lot of different people play a part when it comes to that, especially when you're looking at strategy and how that develops and evolves year to year. And so if you don't have a strategy yet, it's okay. Now is a great time to start and have that conversation with your team. So thank you so much for having me on your podcast.\n\nCole Heilborn (57:10): Yeah, thank you. If folks want to follow along with Rough Wear, if they want to see thousands and thousands of photos of dogs, or if they want to follow along with yourself and maybe see hundreds of photos of your dog, where can people go to connect?\n\nKaim York-Feirn (57:25): We are at Rough Wear on Instagram, lots of pictures of cute dogs and derby dogs. And then you can find me personally on Instagram at Follow the Bear Tracks.\n\nCole Heilborn (57:36): I like that handle. That's cool.\n\nKaim York-Feirn (57:38): Yes. Thank you. It was inspired by my dog.\n\nCole Heilborn (57:42): Ah, nice. Well, Kami, thank you again for the time today. I hope you have a great rest of your day and enjoy the hike. And yeah, who knows, maybe the next time we chat we will be in the metaverse.\n\nKaim York-Feirn (57:54): Yep. Thank you so much, Cole. I'll see you in the Metaverse.\n\nCole Heilborn (57:58): Alright, see you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 621, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Kaim York-Feirn"], "start_timestamp": "55:51", "end_timestamp": "57:58", "podcast_index": 99, "episode_name": "Ep 99: How to Find Your Brand Voice on Social | Kami York-Feirn | Ruffwear", "slug": "ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-99-how-to-find-your-brand-voice-on-social-kami-york-feirn-ruffwear", "guest_name": "Kami York-Feirn", "guest_title": "Social Media Coordinator", "categories": ["digital-marketing"], "short_description": "Social media is constantly changing, new algorithms, new platforms, and new trends are a daily occurrence. As a brand, how do you decide what platforms to be present on? How does your brand communicate on each platform?", "podcast_length": "58:20"}, {"id": "dbc9f172-488b-443e-8a5f-8a2128cc2506", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:00): Welcome to Brand Story. My name is Cole Helebo and I'm going to be your host Today Brand story is all about the marketing professionals who are working in the industry today. The landscape is constantly changing and evolving, takes really great smart people to run a successful brand, and that's what this podcast is all about. It's all about the people behind the brands we interact with, the challenges they face, as well as their personal journey. So if you're interested in learning more about what it takes to run a top brand, I hope you'll love this podcast and find something educational about it. Enjoy today's episode. Well, welcome to Brand Story. Thanks for joining us, everyone. My name is Cole Holborn. I'll be your host today, but it's my pleasure to introduce Jess Anthony. She is the marketing wizard over at ORU Kayak. Jess, how are you doing?\n\nJess Anthony (00:46): I'm doing good. Thanks for having me on here, Cole.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:49): Absolutely. Where are you located?\n\nJess Anthony (00:52): I'm in Oakland right now, working from home. Our office is in Emeryville, which is right between Oakland and Berkeley. Pretty close to the water.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:00): Okay, nice. Yeah, I'm loving the people. Can't see it right now, but I'm loving the DIY sound booth you've got going on. It looks pretty good. So let's first start maybe by talking about the company. Who are you guys? What do you do?\n\nJess Anthony (01:18): Well, our mission statement is transforming how you connect to nature, and that's pretty true. Our products fold, so they transform from box to boat, and they also transform a lot of people's access to the sport of kayaking because you can store them in an apartment, even in a studio apartment, you can stash it under your bed or in a closet. You don't need to have a car to move it or a truck even. You can just get around with a backpack. We take it often on Bart, which is the subway here in the Bay Area.", "token_estimate": 495, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jess Anthony"], "start_timestamp": "00:00", "end_timestamp": "01:18", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "c80440fd-5c36-4fe3-b572-5f80be31be95", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:57): Yeah, and to be clear, we're talking about kayaks, and I think that's the crazy part too, is when you think about, I remember one of your guys', I think your first promo video came out on Kickstarter, and I feel like you guys kind of broke the internet. You're like, what is that thing? That's a folding boat. That's so cool.\n\nJess Anthony (02:14): Yeah, I don't know how long we held that title for, but it was the biggest, I believe, sporting goods Kickstarter for a while. I'm sure somebody beat it by now, but\n\nCole Heilborn (02:26): That's awesome. So tell me a little bit about your role with the company and the marketing department.\n\nJess Anthony (02:32): So I do a lot of our community and content stuff. I go to a lot of our trade shows. I meet with different folks who work at stores to do training on just how to use the product and talk about it, and then just a lot of content management stuff at this point. Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (02:56): Yeah. I feel like the name of the game is content games these days. Excuse me. And you guys are doing a great job with that, and it's cool chatting with you earlier as you kind of broke down the type of content you guys create and how you process that. Personally, I know you guys are a small team. You kind of explain how you guys approach your marketing, maybe from not a granular level, but a more 10,000, 30,000 foot level.\n\nJess Anthony (03:26): So our core team is Mead, and I do a lot of, like I said, the content management. And then our e-commerce guy, Jeffrey, who does, he runs the website and then also does all of our ads. And I mean there's quite a bit of little bits that go into that. And then we also have Alex, R-D-R-C-E-O sits in on that planning, and then Omar, who's our head of customer service, which at our company we view customer service as part of the marketing team.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:05): And why is that? How'd you guys reach that direction or that conclusion?", "token_estimate": 492, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jess Anthony"], "start_timestamp": "01:57", "end_timestamp": "04:05", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "8146c305-a65a-4d57-960a-b207ae2c6262", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Jess Anthony (04:10): I think that customer service gets a really solid view of what people want, which helps our product design team come up with things or do improvements and updates on models. It really helps us understand our messaging, making sure we're getting things out correctly. I like to think of just in general, good marketing is being able to listen. So part of that is being able to listen to your customers. So Omar will collect all the data of what people are reaching out about so that we know where we're missing things or where we have opportunities. One of the big things when we started, we definitely thought we were more of a millennial product or people that are probably in their thirties, forties, and then younger. But baby boomers is definitely an audience we've heard from. And that was first in customer service because that's not who we see on social media as much Facebook, but not as much our other platforms. But customer service data was just letting us know, Hey, this is a big part of your audience. So going from there, that gave us a good sense of maybe how we pivoted our messaging.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:30): Yeah, no, I mean that's a great example I think of listening to your customer base and then implementing changes. And up until this point, how many products have you guys launched?\n\nJess Anthony (05:40): So right now we have five. We had two other models that we used to have an upgraded version of the Bay and the coast that had a little bit more features on it. We found that the difference in features didn't really matter as much to people. They were more concerned with, does it perform all the basic things I want it to do, and is it affordable? So we ended up dropping the higher end model and kind of incorporating a few of those features into our standard bay and coast.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:10): Okay, cool. So for folks that maybe don't know, can you kind of give us the spread of the different products that you have and the differences between all those different kayaks?", "token_estimate": 508, "speakers": ["Jess Anthony", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:10", "end_timestamp": "06:10", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "6aabe3b6-c2d3-49aa-8db9-1eeb30e6980e", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Jess Anthony (06:18): Yeah. So our newest is the Inlet, and that one's really exciting because that's one we've been trying to hit since the very beginning. It answers a lot of those questions. It's super portable. It folds down in just literally three simple steps. The fold down process takes three minutes, and that's for a lot of people who've never touched the boat before, without an instruction manual. When we took it to, or last summer, which was the first public viewing of it, people would just take it and start building it or breaking it down. And it was impressive without asking any questions, they could do it. And we were like, yeah, we hit this one. It's doing what it's supposed to. It also has a little more streamline form when it's in box, when it's in its box, so it's a lot easier to move around. It's only 20 pounds, so you can pack it in the backpack that it comes in, or it comes with, and I'm sorry, it doesn't come with the backpack, but you can get the pack for it and take it in, hike it into an alpine lake for a full weekend with your super lightweight backpacking gear, just slide your tent and\n\nCole Heilborn (07:28): Backpack\n\nJess Anthony (07:28): In. And then we also have, so we kind of split it into two categories, like our rec boats, which are open cockpit. So the inlet is definitely in the rec boat category. And then we also have the Haven, which is our tandem, which converts to a single cedar. And then we have our beach, which is kind of our fun, bigger boat with room. If you want to bring a cooler or some gear, bring your dog. That's a good one for that. And then for our performance models, we have the bay and the coast, and those are designed after a lot of really traditional Arctic kayaks that you would see. And if you think about it, comparing it to biking, it's like the models you can just, they move with your body. They're a little sleeker. You can do really tight turns and higher speeds.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:20): Sure, yeah. Okay. So you got five different products, right? I think you had mentioned that you guys have launched all of them on Kickstarter, right?\n\nJess Anthony (08:30): Yeah, yeah, yeah.", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Jess Anthony", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:18", "end_timestamp": "08:30", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "44c1eca2-ed30-4487-8efa-f3cb10abc015", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (08:33): And like I said, I think that you guys, what was the first one you came out with?\n\nJess Anthony (08:37): So the first one was the original, which is now we call it the Bay ST, but at the time it was just the Oreo kayak.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:45): Okay, gotcha. So walk, I know you haven't been with the company since the beginning, but you guys have had repeated success launching your products on Kickstarter. What do you think it takes, I guess, what's your guys' secret sauce, if you will, to bringing those products to the market through a successful Kickstarter campaign?\n\nJess Anthony (09:05): Yeah, I think it's having a good story is first and then being able to tell it. Well, the first version of the story you write is never what you put up. So it's a lot of editing, simplifying, and just being able to show it in photos and videos, which actually for our product is a little tricky because when we talk about what is a single still image, we don't really have one because there's the box and there's the boat. So a lot of times video or a photo that shows both kind of gets that point across. But I would say also one of the most important things for a successful Kickstarter launch is getting good content. So hire a photographer or a videographer. If you have budget, it's worth it. And then spend a lot of your time on writing about your products. No one knows it better than you, and then have everybody ask, read it and give you feedback. And then the other thing I would say is hire a PR firm if you can do that. We worked with Purple Orange on our last Kickstarter, which was our most successful, and they really helped us push that forward. They helped us find different awards we could apply for, and we won quite a bit. And that comes with a lot of a attention along the way. So they were just really great to work with as far as getting it in front of more people.", "token_estimate": 477, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jess Anthony"], "start_timestamp": "08:33", "end_timestamp": "09:05", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "5b5fa6a3-17c2-43c2-bb7b-aff58bce4af2", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:32): Yeah. Yeah. Kickstarter and Indiegogo. And I feel like there's a few other platforms that I'm forgetting that the whole Kickstarter thing, they seem to be getting really, really popular, and there's been some really successful brands that have been launched. Do you think the Kickstarter model will become, I don't know, will it continue to grow or do you think it's kind of plateaued with where it's at?\n\nJess Anthony (10:57): I think it'll definitely stay in use for quite a while. I think one thing that's nice about it is that it's an audience of people who are really excited to support new ideas, and I think that's a little bit of this idea that anybody can be an entrepreneur. I think it's not necessarily that simple, but I do think that it does come with a lot of getting it in front of an audience who would be excited and then possibly even support your venture. For us as a bigger company who is maybe a little more established than some of the other Kickstarter campaigns, you may see some of the benefits for us are we get to gauge interest in it, which is really helpful when we're talking to some of our big accounts, this is how much interest we got on Kickstarter. We think you guys will want more or less, you want your retailers to be able to sell through all of your product. (11:56): So that's been really helpful for us, helping them figure out what to order. It's also really been helpful in just covering the cost of manufacturing. Manufacturing. It's pretty expensive to set up, and then you have a couple months before you can even start selling your product. So knowing that people have already put money down and are agreeing to buy it makes it feel a little better when you go to the bank and you're like, Hey, we need a loan to get this up and running. And then they're like, oh, we see the money's there. So yeah, so that's another benefit.\n\nCole Heilborn (12:29): Sure, sure. Yeah. Interesting. So tell me a little bit more about your story. How'd you end up with Oro?", "token_estimate": 507, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jess Anthony"], "start_timestamp": "10:32", "end_timestamp": "12:29", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "e343ab59-e079-4cc2-9c22-719e9db2f5a3", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Jess Anthony (12:38): Well, I was actually working next door and I got to know everybody mostly because where the office I was in did the receiving for everyone in the building, and we had really big boxes coming in that said they were kayaks, but they didn't look like kayak shapes or they weren't really big enough to be kayaks. So I was just really curious. So I started chatting with some of the folks who were working at oru, and I was just like, these are cool. Can I see one? How does it actually fit in this box? And then they let me borrow one for a weekend, and I was pretty hooked. (13:12): I felt like it would be an inflatable kayak where you get out on the water and it's fun and you can be on the water, but it definitely had a lot more performance value. It just cuts the water better than a lot of traditional kayaks that are kayak kayaks, not even inflatables. So I was pretty impressed. And then I started chatting more and the job I had was just a contract job, so I was wrapping that up and I was planning to go to Alaska for a month and not work, just go hiking and stuff. And then they were like, oh, we need somebody to help in customer service. Can you help? And I was like, well, I can help for two weeks and then I'm out of here. And they were like, well, if you want to work whenever you can from Alaska, we'll give you a pair of 16 foot touring kayaks to take with you and you can just work when you're available. So I said yes, because a pretty awesome offer.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:12): Yeah, no kidding.\n\nJess Anthony (14:13): That was how I got in. And then eventually I started doing a lot of events and community stuff and then joined the marketing team last April.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:22): Gotcha. So what's the future look like for Aura? Where are you guys going? How do you want to evolve in the marketing side of the business?", "token_estimate": 465, "speakers": ["Jess Anthony", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "12:38", "end_timestamp": "14:22", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "d675b429-2864-4043-82ee-3aa8824c12f3", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Jess Anthony (14:32): That's a good question. This year has been really interesting. I feel like we're still sorting out what the future looks like, but it's certainly been a year where you have to try a lot of things that you wouldn't do. Usually this time of year I'm on the road traveling quite a bit, doing stuff, working with REI for their employee training events and going to trade shows and things like that. So none of that's happening this year. So we're kind of focusing a lot more on digital and other ways to talk to people, which is really interesting. Some of it we're finding is doing really well, so it'll probably be something that gets incorporated long term and it is just got us rethinking where we put our energies right now. We don't have a solid answer yet. I feel like everything's going to continue to change for at least the rest of this year.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:30): That's fair.\n\nJess Anthony (15:32): But I guess I'm excited. The design team is working on some new ideas and I'm not sure when those will come out yet, but I'm really, really excited to do another campaign whenever that happens.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:49): Yeah. Well no, I'm excited to see what you guys come out with next. One of the things we had chatted about earlier on our call was just about the idea of outdoor access for everyone. And I know that's something that you're really passionate about and something you're trying to figure out how you guys can do at oru, and I feel like we were talking about how ORU is accomplishing that and what things you guys could be doing better in the future, but tell me a little more about that passion and where does that come from and how do you want to help people?", "token_estimate": 427, "speakers": ["Jess Anthony", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "14:32", "end_timestamp": "15:49", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "cc6f891f-51b2-4b94-a4f1-b806f301fddc", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Jess Anthony (16:23): That's kind of open-ended in general. I am a fan of increasing equity wherever possible, which is interesting to do in marketing because that's sometimes the opposite thing, but you do have some resources. I think you have a platform for communication for ideas to move to spotlight work other people are doing and to contribute to projects they're doing. We've definitely had folks reach out for boats that want to do projects that are either fundraising or just different things like that. That is something I'm looking to do is finding ways we can contribute back through different spotlighting people doing good work and help encourage other people that are within our community to donate and just see what they're doing. (17:13): Originally this summer we were going to do some local events that would open up free paddling to anybody who wanted to come. So I was kind of excited about that and I was also hoping to find ways that we could partner with local groups in the fall that were either nonprofits or arts groups to use the kayaks. It's a pretty cool, the bay is pretty cool, and I think I mentioned when we were on the phone last time, almost every city is built on water and they're there because the water was there. So agriculture worked. So then there's a city and now the water isn't something that we really use. It used to be one of the biggest forms of transportation for goods, but now most individuals don't really access it that often. Just opening up ways for people to see our city that way again would be really cool.\n\nCole Heilborn (18:05): Yeah. What is being on the water? What does it do for you? What does it mean to you?", "token_estimate": 419, "speakers": ["Jess Anthony", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "16:23", "end_timestamp": "18:05", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "e88dba9e-6677-4369-86b5-66ab77babce2", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Jess Anthony (18:12): It's actually one of my favorite parts of the job. I was testing a new design idea that we're working on earlier this week, and I was out there by myself, social distancing, and I was like, this is my favorite day of the week. I am out on the water and I'm just out here kayaking and trying this out and it's beautiful. So I love that. I also love people. I love chatting and getting to know. For me, kayaking is one thing, and then for anybody else, it's their own thing. A lot of us have memories of growing up doing it, and then a lot of people are trying it for the very first time when I meet them. So I really like learning those stories and just thinking about what it means to different people.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:00): Yeah, and it's cool that you guys connect people to that connection, and I think you do it in a way that opens it up for other people too, because one of the biggest selling points, or not the biggest, but one of the selling points about your kayaks is just like they fold up. So you can live in an urban city in a small flat and shove it in a closet and then pull it out and be on the water, and that wasn't something that urban dwellers had readily access to.\n\nJess Anthony (19:28): Yeah, that kind of accessibility is pretty cool. And it actually applies to a lot more people than I would've thought when I was working in customer service. We had folks reach out who had back injuries and had been very active in certain types of sports that weren't able to do them anymore independently. And a lot of people found the Oreo because they were like, oh, it's lightweight. I don't need to be able to put weight above my head. I can pull it out of the back of my SUV or my trunk and get it to the dock and set it up all by myself without anybody having to be around, which is pretty cool to hear that it gives people that independence that you don't need to ask somebody to help you.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:12): Yeah. Yeah. That's cool. What would you like to see change in the outdoor industry?", "token_estimate": 510, "speakers": ["Jess Anthony", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:12", "end_timestamp": "20:12", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "69bee738-c295-4efc-aaf8-9e0f4df69afd", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Jess Anthony (20:19): Interesting. Well, I've been to, or the last two, I went last summer and then this winter, and I got to attend some really cool talks. And I think there's some interesting, I don't dunno if they're projects or coalitions that are being built, but I think or is doing a good job of fostering that or at least starting those conversations, but just helping create access to the outdoors for more people who don't necessarily have that. And some of those coalitions are coming from health insurance companies. And then I think LL Bean was one of 'em that partnered with a whole city to try and make parks accessible for some people, their neighborhood parks aren't safe, and that's a barrier to going outdoors and just adapting what outdoors is. I think it could be your city park and it could be your mountain range that's a three hour driver away. I think considering both of those things outdoors is maybe something I like seeing and I've been seeing more of.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:34): Oh, interesting.\n\nJess Anthony (21:35): Yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (21:36): I never thought about that before, but you mentioned that and it jogs a memory. I read an article and I can't remember where, I feel like it was in Colorado, but there's some city park on a river and they've opened up recreation camping for families. Are you familiar with this\n\nJess Anthony (21:54): Idea? No, not this specifically.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:56): Okay. Well, but it leads into that idea, like we say, the outdoors and we have this idea of remote distant Alaska, but sometimes the outdoors for some people is just like their park. And that's an interesting shift in thinking that maybe needs to happen in order to get more people outside.", "token_estimate": 431, "speakers": ["Jess Anthony", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:19", "end_timestamp": "21:56", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "a9248e1f-8dd4-4a14-bfe8-783de9adb769", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Jess Anthony (22:18): Yeah, I think, I mean, I am queer and I feel like I present queer and there's definitely been places where I didn't feel safe. And I think part of being able to get people outdoors is people feeling safe. And if you don't see anyone that looks like you, I think it's very hard to feel like you're welcome somewhere, even if everybody there is like, oh, cool, there's another person. That's fine. So I guess in that respect, representation matters and I think that's something that the outdoors industry can contribute for everybody. And I guess I've been seeing a little bit more of that, but I think it's exciting. I like seeing more of it, and I think it does make a big difference to see yourself represented using a product and just having a good time.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:09): How does it make you feel when you look at an environment and you see people who are like you and you feel like you can, there's not that awkward barrier of entry. Speaking personally, how do you feel when you see those scenarios,\n\nJess Anthony (23:27): When you see no awkward barrier\n\nCole Heilborn (23:31): Or maybe drawing on personal experiences where you have felt that awkward barrier?\n\nJess Anthony (23:34): Yeah, I think when there's no awkward barrier, you don't feel anything, you're going on your hike or whatever, it feels good. And I think that's what a lot of people, especially people really involved in the outdoors industry feel when they go to most parks. I think when you do feel it, you're just not sure if you feel welcome or if you feel safe. I grew up in Kansas and I've done a lot of road trips all around the country, and I think there have been places where I've felt people look at me and I did not feel necessarily safe or welcome. And usually it's fine for me in every case. It's been fine, but it's just like when you're not sure, you're like, maybe we'll go to the next park. Try it later.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:20): Yeah. Huh. Yeah. Well, no, that's cool. And I appreciate you sharing your backstory. I think that helps provide context for why you care about the things you do care about. Everyone's story kind of shapes what we do in our future.", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Jess Anthony", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:18", "end_timestamp": "24:20", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "e51c1aff-52ff-41f4-974a-759b2589624b", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Jess Anthony (24:35): Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:37): So I feel like you're the type of person with all these adventures. Going to Alaska Road trips, you probably have some pretty cool stories. If you could tell one crazy adventure story around a campfire, what would it be?\n\nJess Anthony (24:50): Oh, let's see. Let me think on that for a second. I guess probably a boat trip in Croatia.\n\nCole Heilborn (25:01): Oh, okay.\n\nJess Anthony (25:03): I grew up canoeing and kayaking, so that's what I'm used to. But I grew up in Kansas, which is mostly rivers and lakes and pretty slow moving ones for the most part. So I was out on the Adriatic and they were like, do you know how to navigate the sea? And I was like, oh yeah, this will be fine. But the truth was I didn't, and there's also, I think it was maybe a cultural thing. Several people were like, oh, go to this big island. That's where everybody likes to go and everyone likes to go in the morning. But I was like, oh, I want to sleep in and I don't want to go where everyone is. So I went to the little far away island and in the afternoon, but apparently there was a three o'clock wind that comes up every day and the boat was really small and there were these massive white caps and I thought we were going to flip over. I had white knuckles the whole way back, and I got back and the guy was like, oh, you went there and you made it back at this time. Nobody does that. I'm surprised I didn't have to come find you. And I was like, oh, okay.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:10): Oh, that's awesome. Well, you made it back safe and sound.\n\nJess Anthony (26:13): Yeah. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:15): Great. Well, if people want to find you, if they want to find oru Kayak, where can people follow along?\n\nJess Anthony (26:22): So we're at oru kayak.com. You can find me on LinkedIn and you can, if you want to shoot us a message on Instagram or LinkedIn or wherever, info at oru kayak is always a good spot to reach out. That'd be great. We love hearing from people.", "token_estimate": 503, "speakers": ["Jess Anthony", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "24:35", "end_timestamp": "26:22", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "1c1352d0-7787-4770-baa9-d63d54efce7e", "episode_id": "3179413a-a697-41eb-b8d2-3f6773273e79", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c482b", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (26:44): Awesome. For the folks listening, if you have any comments about an ORU kayak, feel free to shoot Jess and her team a message and they'll take care of it. But Jess, thank you very much. I'll let you get back to your day and get ready for your weekend travels or stay in Cook in whatever. But thank you for being on the show.\n\nJess Anthony (27:04): Yeah, thanks for chatting. It was nice.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:07): Yeah. Alright, well take care and we'll chat with you later.\n\nJess Anthony (27:09): Sounds good.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:11): Bye. Okay, bye. Thank you for listening to Brand Story. If you enjoyed this episode, I encourage you to share it with a friend or family member who also might enjoy. And please tune in next time for the next episode. Thank you.", "token_estimate": 196, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jess Anthony"], "start_timestamp": "26:44", "end_timestamp": "27:11", "podcast_index": 9, "episode_name": "Ep 9: Jess Anthony |Kickstarter Success and Finding Equality in the Outdoors | Oru Kaya‪k‬", "slug": "ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-9-jess-anthony-kickstarter-success-and-finding-equality-in-the-outdoors-oru-kayak", "guest_name": null, "guest_title": null, "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think you have a platform for communication, for ideas to move, to spotlight work other people are doing, and to contribute to projects they're doing.\"", "podcast_length": "44:31"}, {"id": "69463611-a3f3-4f65-a77f-5cdf87715680", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Darren Josey (00:00:02): And so when I came to nemo, I took my running lessons and how often I was talking to that customer. If we're looking to talk to our customer only when they're going to buy something, we're not going to be talking to them that often. So I think just the shrewd business point of view is that NEMO sells durable gear and we need to find other ways to talk to our customer because they're not always going to be looking to buy something that's just not the nature of what we sell.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:31): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Holborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole, he Warren, and today I'm sitting down with Darren Josie. He is the VP of Marketing over at NEMO Equipment. Darren, how are you?\n\nDarren Josey (00:01:13): I'm feeling great. It's a sunny day in Malden, Massachusetts. Can't beat it.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:18): Well, I appreciate you taking the time to join me on the show today. I'm glad you're here and I'm stoked we can chat you. Sounds like you had a good weekend.", "token_estimate": 394, "speakers": ["Darren Josey", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00:02", "end_timestamp": "00:01:18", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "69249228-684d-4797-9d58-f62bc21c6e0b", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Darren Josey (00:01:26): It was an excellent weekend and got a safe fan of the pod of when I found it. I was kind of geeking out to my wife, who was also a marketer. I was like, I found my niche, an outdoor marketing podcast, and she was like, okay, very interesting. She's in tech. She thinks what we do is funny, and I think what she do is she does is funny. But yeah, had a great weekend and it was sunny. Got to spend some time, some great quality family town with my daughter and my wife. Got some alone time plus some really fun family time. We planted some tomatoes, which is exciting. We're trying our hand at some indoor gardening. I'm really just trying to keep the plants we already have alive, so this is another step of, let's see how we do here.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:02:09): Yeah. So, okay, this is a totally random question, but you introduced the idea, you talked about the show and it being a show for outdoor marketers, which is obviously our whole deal here. There's lots of other marketing podcasts out there. And I'm curious, do you feel like having a podcast specific to an industry like the outdoors or tech, there's different value to be gleaned from those different industries that are applicable?", "token_estimate": 305, "speakers": ["Darren Josey", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:01:26", "end_timestamp": "00:02:09", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "9515d1c3-8774-4c95-84db-3683358fea02", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Darren Josey (00:02:34): Yeah, I think so. Well, what I feel on the tech side, because my wife is in the industry, their overhead is people. I've come from consumer goods companies where we have to spend a lot of money on making the physical goods on the tech side, they don't. So some of the companies that she's worked for, they have 1230 person marketing groups and agencies so they can test out a lot more ideas and concepts that we just don't have the luxury of doing. I mean, in many ways we're so far behind what tech marketing is doing, and at the same time, I think the consumer goods tends to have a wider audience that understands what we're doing. So I really rely on her to keep me in the loop of what she is working on, and that's made me a much better marketer. But I love to hear what other brands are doing, and I'm a big fan of this podcast here, and I love hearing what other really smart folks are talking about because I really try to be humble as I've gone through my career. What I've learned from some pretty influential folks that I've worked with is that that humility is going to make you smarter and a better, not just a better person, but a better at your everyday job too.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:03:49): Yeah, interesting. Well, that's a whole nother subject. That's actually not what we're here to talk about, but we're going to have to table that to come back to that idea. Well, Darren, I'd love if you could give us a quick background on nemo, a little introduction to yourself, and just to give folks an idea, we're going to be discussing this idea of building an non-transactional relationship with your customer since you've stepped into the role at nemo. Sounds like there's some pretty exciting projects going on, and I'm really excited to kind of dive into your philosophy behind this idea. But let's start with a little background info.", "token_estimate": 469, "speakers": ["Darren Josey", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02:34", "end_timestamp": "00:03:49", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "d547a4e6-1b78-40ce-b4f2-777a52fb9b7e", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Darren Josey (00:04:21): Sure. So I am a 13 year outdoor industry veteran, which when I started my career in the outdoor industry, I didn't even really think about an outdoor industry. I was just working as a footwear manager at City Sports in Boston trying to not work the retail floor anymore. I was tired of working weekends and holidays and not having consecutive days off and working retail pre the great recession back in oh eight. I mean, man, what a crazy ride that was. But I am very thankful for the lessons I learned there. I mean, if you can sell shoes and gear to people face to face, I mean I still take those lessons for me everywhere. It was a great foundation in my career. But I had a friend who was by the name of Georgia Shaw, who was a former city sports alum who came back into my store and a busy Saturday morning and said, Hey, you want to interview for a customer service job at brm? (00:05:20): And I was like, yes, please, please get me out of retail. I don't want to do this anymore. And interviewed there and was one of two customer service reps in 2009, right as the five Fingers revolution was taking off. That was a fantastic time to take a lot of the lessons I learned from the running and fitness world coming out of city sports and grow into this awesome new industry to me of the outdoor industry. And that job really started me on a path of being someone who recreates outside. I had a coworker who asked me to go rock climbing. I didn't know what rock climbing was, and I just sounded cool. So I was like, yeah, lemme try it. And we went to a gym two towns away from where I grew up and I was hooked instantaneously. And I remember coming back to the office and going, whoa, all these rock climbing magazines are everywhere. (00:06:19): I just didn't see it. It wasn't until I experienced it, I hadn't seen it and it was all around me and I felt like, how come no one told me about this? I could have been doing this. This is so much fun. And that just led me to my sports today. I mean, I rock climb, I surf, I snowboard, I camp, I hike, and I really thank that first job and my time there working in customer service, sales, operations, logistics. I mean, I worked with the head of sales and our CEO Tony Post looking at our sell-through every week, compiling all that sell-through data from REI to our specialty retailers to help organize our buys. That was really, really fun. And I treasure my time there. And then I got a great opportunity. I was interviewing at Black Diamond and Petzel. I had fallen in love with the state of Utah. (00:07:12): I was just head over heels of climbing and actually fishing and trail running out there and then also snowboarding. I was interviewing out there and asked my former CEO Tony to be a reference for my interview process, and he said, I got your reference right here. I'm starting a shoot company. You want to come with me? And I was like, that sounds way better. I want to do that. So I got to help Tony and a few of the folks start the Topo Athletic brand, which is really fun and still thriving today. And that was another set of amazing lessons to start something I still remember, we spent about a year getting that brand ready to be launched and on launch day, the only people in the world that knew about that brand were the four people who were starting it and our significant others. (00:07:57): And that's it. And it was something I totally took for granted coming from brm that was a family owned brand that had been around since 1937. I might get a little rusty my ber years there, but that was such a different challenge and to make decisions to keep the lights on, to start grassroots marketing where you see one pair of shoes pop up in a neighborhood, three pairs of shoes pop up in a neighborhood. I mean, that was absolutely incredible. And I got to be a sales rep there, go town to town, work with our independent reps, but also be a rep and in-house rep in the southeast and learn, spend a lot of time traveling around this country. But another opportunity popped up, a close friend working at Polar Tech who was the head of sales, just invited me out to see the mighty, mighty Bosstones with what happened to be the executive team of Polar Tech. (00:08:50): And we had a great time with that show. I'm I guess a secret ska person. I love ska. I hope it comes back. I don't think that's going to happen though. But we had just enjoyed the concert and couple drinks came back home after that to see that at the Outdoor Retailer Show came back and the North American marketing manager, I put in her two weeks and my buddy said, Hey, what about my friend Darren? Do you think you should interview for this role? And the CEO was like, yeah, have him interview. I walked out of that interview after almost three hours, was like, I have to have this job. And went full-time into marketing there. Did about five years at Polar Tech where I could to focus on over a dozen different industries from fashion to action sports to flame retardant markets, so oil and gas, electrical, military. (00:09:42): We got to start two different direct to consumer brands in Polar Fleece, which was all made in New England brand. Every single pullover was fully customized to a military direct business. And that was fantastic. I mean, man, to be part of a big company and really sit back and have conversations with all those different creators in marketing for all those different industries. And then we got bought by a company called Milliken, another family owned brand, and that was fantastic, that Milliken organization really well run, multi-billion dollar enterprise that was looking to really grow and flew our whole marketing team down to their headquarters to South Carolina on one of two private jets. And I sat there with the CEO of Milliken and said, and he told us we need to take risks with young talent and leadership. We're going to grow this brand and we've never done marketing, so we want to learn from the polar tech marketing team. (00:10:42): And I was sitting in a hotel with a colleague and I said, I think I'm going to spend the rest of my life here. This is fantastic. And then the pandemic hit, and being that far back in the supply chain, that polar tick was, it really took a toll on the company. And we had a pretty big furlough and then layoff. And I got to say, I'm so fortunate for that time, I had a brand new kid, my wife had just changed jobs. It was fairly hectic. We had to go with daddy daycare, but I treasured the time that I got to spend with my daughter for five months. It was just the two of us locked down and my wife working in the house and going through a new job, which was really hard because that job went a hundred percent remote day one. (00:11:26): But it gave me the time to think about my own values and wanting to work for a company that shared those values. And when a recruiter reached out to me for an opportunity at nemo, I said, well, let's have the call. And I talked to Cam, who was the founder and CEO and majority owner. And he had gone through his own kind of value searching. It had always been part of the NEMO brand, but they hadn't really talked about it a lot. And he wanted to make sure that whoever he would hire to lead the marketing team also shared the values for the brand. And I actually got to work as a contractor for three months at the end of 2020. And at the end of that time, he and I both felt this was a great fit and I don't think I'll ever get that opportunity again. (00:12:16): I don't think the business world would allow people to work three months somewhere and make sure it's a good fit. But I feel really good, really fortunate I got that opportunity. And then yeah, I got to lead the team and build a marketing team starting day one of 2021, and it's been just over a year, started as a senior director and now VP of marketing. So that's my career. Thanks man. It's been a ride and I've absolutely loved it. I love the people I work with at every job. So far this has been a very welcoming industry and the sports that I participate in. Same", "token_estimate": 2031, "speakers": ["Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:04:21", "end_timestamp": "00:04:21", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "75526986-4052-4f5b-ae3b-ba3b24918a28", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:12:53): Well. So I got to imagine the challenge that folks face, and I'm sure you guys face every day, is how to engage your customer without always being there, selling them something. This idea that just because you're interacting with your consumer doesn't mean you have to be pushing product down their throat or expecting something. Where did this idea come from and how has this kind of manifested, at least in terms of the challenges that you see at NEMO compared to some of your past roles?", "token_estimate": 129, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:12:53", "end_timestamp": "00:12:53", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "c348fe6c-7a1f-48e2-9f60-45869c8daa50", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Darren Josey (00:13:24): Gotcha. I go back to being on the shoe floor back at city Sports. I remember talking to different reps and brands and our corporate team understanding the buying process of your core running customer. That customer was coming in every 250 to 300 miles to buy a new pair of shoes. And it was important for everyone in the shoe floor to understand you're going to see that person again. So understand how you're positioning a certain product, understand they're going to come back here to probably get a watch or a pair of shorts. So it was really quick. I mean you could see someone once every four months to maybe eight months, but multiple times a year you'd see the same runners who lived in the area. When I got to NEMO, understanding our products. So I started camping in 2010. Part of my privilege in life is that I was raised by a Puerto Rican mom and a black dad who told me that you're going to enter a lot of spaces in life where you are the only person who looks like you here. So have the courage to go try something even if you're the only one doing it. But do be weary of that. You're walking into maybe that you're the only person like you. So that privilege allowed me when someone gifted me a tent and two sleeping bags when I worked at brm to say, I'm just going to try camping. (00:14:49): That was 2010. I still have that camping gear that is 12-year-old camping gear that I was using up until last year. And so when I came to nemo, I took my running lessons and how often I was talking to that customer and I looked at my own camping experience and said, well, my gear has lasted 12 years. I camped probably three to five times a year. I take really good care of my gear. I also loan it out to friends and family. So if we're looking to talk to our customer owner when they're going to buy something, we're not going to be talking to them that often. So I think just the shrewd business point of view is that NEMO sells durable gear and we need to find other ways to talk to our customer because they're not always going to be looking to buy something that's just not the nature of what we sell. (00:15:36): So I think that's the pure dollars and cents. The other reason we want to be doing this and creating that non-transactional relationship, I think is understanding or having a wider view on sustainability. So if I go back to my first time camping, being gifted two sleeping bags in a tent, I dragged my, at the time, girlfriend out on a camping trip. We had no idea that you needed a sleeping pad. I just got in the bag and laid down on the rocky Rudy ground and was like, I guess this is camping. That's it, right? I mean, I think I drank enough be I didn't really feel the ground that much, but I'm the kind of person that if I have just a little bit of fun, I'll come back and I'll keep trying to do it again and again. But that's not everybody out there. (00:16:26): And I really try to take my personal experience as well as feedback from other events and interactions I've had with people out there in the outdoor industry. I think there's really three types of people out there and that's why we want to have this non-transactional relationship. I think the first type of person is the person who just hears about an activity and wants to try it. They don't need to see anyone like 'em doing it just that sounds cool, I want to try it. And that's me. I had to learn a lot of hard lessons, not exactly, well, I certainly got hurt bouldering without a pad, something else. I had to learn a hard lesson. But I think that person just needs to see a little bit of spark, a little bit of fun. They're going to keep coming back over and over again. The second group of folks, which I think a lot of people fall into are they need to see someone who looks like them to know this is something our community does and this is safe to do. (00:17:29): I think we want to have resources for these folks as well as that first group so that they keep coming back and have a really positive experience right away to go, okay, this is safe. We can do this, and then let's not let people struggle. I think we as an outdoor industry have maybe taken for granted for too long that we've presented images of a person and an amazing place with some gear and said, you'll just find yourself there. Well, that's just not true. I think there's some folks that will put in the time to really try to search, search will take some slam doors in their face and go, I'm going to keep looking. But there's a lot of people that just won't do that. Or if they have that one bad experience because someone put 'em on something that was too steep or had them go out on a day that was a little bit too big surfing and they almost drowned or felt like they almost drowned, they're not going back. (00:18:22): And so that group, we really want to make sure that we have a lot of resources available to them. And we've done that actually with our adventure resources page, which I can talk about in a bit. And then the last group, that third group are the folks that say, no, we don't do this. And if you do this, you're not one of us. The best way that I could explain that to anyone listening from my own community, I've heard this from both friends and family, is that's some white people. Shit. I still get that to this day. Why are you trying to do these white activities? Well, the best way to address that is to show people of different communities and call them out by name. You can't say only white people do that. If you know me and I'm a person of color and I do this and I'm an outdoor evangelist, I want to bring more people out here. (00:19:15): Now, we don't want to sugarcoat and say, well, racism or sexism or classism doesn't exist in the outdoor world. It absolutely does. It is not nowhere in our society's immune to that. But this is something that is accessible for people of all groups, some easier than others, but this is public land that is made available to the public, and we want to address that group with people of that community and frankly find more leaders of outdoor organizations that can represent and talk directly to that community. And we've tried to set that up with a few of our partnerships this year with NEMO to talk to these groups with representation of that community to them say, Hey, this is a safe space. Here's a place that you can go. And at a minimum, an absolute minimum, you can say, you now know someone of the community that does these things, and if you want to sign a permission slip for your kid, think about me and I'm out there because that's really this group. (00:20:12): Maybe some of them are a little bit older. I don't think that's for us. And if you can change their mind, they will then sign that permission slip so that their kid can go out and try that. I mean, I still remember convincing my parents to go on the seventh grade ski trip, which was such a big deal. I didn't know what skiing was. I only knew that was one night away from my parents, so I wanted to do it, but they were like, sure, we'll sign that. And if we could just win over those folks to let their kids do it, that's a huge win for our industry.", "token_estimate": 1802, "speakers": ["Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:13:24", "end_timestamp": "00:13:24", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "0a295294-e231-44ab-8737-7c798950b9b1", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:20:40): Yeah, yeah, well said. So obviously this idea of building on transactional relationship, there's so much more beneath the surface that bubbles up to the top around this idea. But I guess how had NEMO been approaching this challenge prior and now since you have come into the role, I know you have a bunch of different initiatives and a bunch of different ways that you're trying to create this non-transactional relationship.\n\nDarren Josey (00:21:10): No doubt. I think the way, well, let me speak to Nemo's past. So Cam will proudly tell anyone that NEMO has spent more dollars on sustainability than it has on marketing in the 20 years of this brand existing. That work is really hard to do and it's hard to, our tagline is adventure anywhere, adventure anywhere for anyone forever. And it's hard to adventure anywhere when we don't have a planet to adventure on. So investing in sustainability, it's a long road and dollars spent over the last 20 years have led to really innovative products that improve the performance for people and planet. So that's where NEMO had spent its dollars and time before since hiring me. One of the things I pitched the brand on when I was a contractor was building out these adventure resources to establish that non-transactional relationship. So when you hit our homepage, yes, you see camping gear, but right away you're going to see our adventure resources ribbon. (00:22:16): We want to inspire people, giving them some great videos, recipes, just stories to latch onto and get them stoked. We also want to show some education with these camping by type landing pages. Again, I go back to that my first camping trip, I didn't know you needed a sleeping pad. Showing people with these pages what a campsite can actually look like is really powerful. All the different pieces of kit that you need to create something really beautiful because you never know who you're talking to. You could be potentially, a customer could be looking your site trying to convince a significant other or a friend to go camping. And if all they know is well, when I went camping with my family, or all I've seen is a mildewy table with some cold hot dogs, that's not what I'm interested in. So to show that camping can be super luxurious to show that camping can be simple but still very comfortable, is really important and a good educational aspect of like, whoa, I didn't know it was all these pieces and you don't need all of them. (00:23:22): And we kind of break 'em down by something more beginner, intermediate, or really versatile to something really, really luxurious to we want to provide some guides to help take you out there. I'm someone who, I've hired a couple guides in my life and it's been really, really empowering to go outside with someone. The way guides are set up is they want you to be able to do it on your own afterwards. So they're going to show you a great time, but also teach you some things so that you can go out and do it afterwards. And that's really what we as an industry need. And I think a big reason why NEMO was really excited to take on this challenge and build up these adventure resources is that this is a special time in our country's history, right? Anywhere from, if you look at the OIA report, it says over 7 million people have started recreating since the pandemic. (00:24:18): I looked at a K report that said 10 million people. So anywhere between seven to 10 million people started recreating outside. And about a third of those folks said they're not going to do it after the pandemic ends. Well in 2022, it seems like fingers crossed we could be going towards endemic instead of continued pandemic. So this is a special moment for our industry to try to hold on to all of those folks who have newly joined us. But at the same time, for our core audience who's been recreating, I think we've all been recreating more than we ever have before. And so let's inspire these folks to try a new space. So we have, as actually part of our adventure resources, we name the locations of our brand shoots. We worked really closely with the land managers to make sure that these areas were ready, willing, and capable to bring on more traffic. (00:25:09): The A MC was our partner for our backpacking brand shoot, but we're going to continue to build out more potential trips to inspire folks because if you've been doing this for a while, your regular trail, your regular campsites have become busier. So why not try something new, try a gem that you haven't seen before or try camping in a different season? We actually, we're going to be launching some kinesiology tips and guides from one of our ambassadors, Tristan Ho, who's a trained kinesiologist as well as a mountaineer and ultralight through Hiker to get people like a cold weather training guide as well as a beginner, intermediate and expert kinesiology backpacking training guide for people. So we really want to invest in adventure resources for anyone from a beginner backpacker to an experienced car camper so that everyone can either start or continue on a lifetime journey of adventure.", "token_estimate": 1287, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:20:40", "end_timestamp": "00:21:10", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "19ce038b-07d0-4b58-a5bc-2064facce1b6", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:26:07): You said something interesting that, so seven or 10 million, seven to 10 million new people have been getting outside, but only a third of those people are projected to continue after the pandemic. Interesting. So two thirds of those people are not going to continue,\n\nDarren Josey (00:26:24): And that's pretty scary. We don't get, I mean this is a once in a lifetime opportunity to have millions of people start recreating outside. And one of the big things that I've experienced, and in talking to other Bipoc folks in the industry is that a of us didn't have friends and family who wanted to do these things with us. And one of the big parts of the Adventure Resources page is finding other people to go with. So we've partnered with Latinx hikers loosely tma, one of the co-founders of Latinx hikers is one of the models for our brand shoot. And so we did a little mini documentary about her as well as we have a bunch of links to tell more people about Latinx hikers, whether you're an ally and you want to donate and support it, or you are a Latinx person or ally and you want to go with them on a trip, we want people to find these resources as well as another company called Camp Yoshi, two black brothers. (00:27:25): Their company's based out of Portland, Oregon now, but I believe they're both from the Mid-Atlantic. What I loved about that partnership is that they show black excellence as in camping, and they told us in our initial conversations about 70% of their clientele are first time campers who are coming. Some of them come as groups, some of come by themselves and they are spending 2,500 to $3,500 on an all-inclusive camping trip. That is so different than other conversations I've had to sit in my years in this industry. When people talk about being more inclusive, like, well, if we're going to be more inclusive, we have to make things cheaper. Well take a look at Campi. They are a thriving business who is selling all inclusive top of the line pricing for first time campers. Now, don't get me wrong, they have some experience folks too, but to say that if you want to bring in more people of color into your activity, you got to make it cheaper. (00:28:23): This goes directly in the face of that. You can't just say one community group only looks a certain way or only has X amount money to spend. This is a great example that there are more flavors or more income brackets within the black community. So pretty excited with that one. And then the last, but certain not least, I'm so proud to say a college classmate of mine, crystal is a co-founder of a company called Inclusive Journeys. Now, inclusive Journeys answers one of the toughest questions out there, and I get this question all the time. Where is a safe space for me to recreate? And what they've created is the Inclusive Guide, which is essentially the Digital Green book. They're like a woke Yelp. But a key difference is that not only do they provide ratings for different community groups of underrepresented and marginalized communities, but they also offer business training for places of work that didn't get a great review or interested in training to make sure their staff and their areas of a place of business is more inclusive. (00:29:33): So it's not just, oh, you got a bad review or you got a good review and you want to get a great review. They offer training to help that business actually improve, which I really, really love. I mean, we live in a world now where, I mean there's ratings on everything, but is there an answer to actually make the business better? Is there a way to actually make it more inclusive and they offer that as well. So those are three of the partnerships we have around our Find people and places to Go, which is such an important part of our resource that a lot of people are asking for.", "token_estimate": 964, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:26:07", "end_timestamp": "00:26:24", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "789d0521-12e1-4ad2-ab40-cefedd2aee71", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:30:02): So a couple of things that stand out to me there, I'm curious to hear your thoughts on. I think you touched on the idea of, so your models, your ambassadors are people that are helping get people outside and not only do they look the part, but they're also doing it in their day to day. And people, your audience, your customers can connect with those people and go have those experiences, but you're also tagging and making the locations where you shoot very public, which folks who are listening, probably everyone's heard of the Taboo like Instagram, do I tag it? Do I not tag it situation? And here you guys are promoting these spaces, but it sounds like you're also communicating with the land managers to ensure that these spaces could receive more capacity and not to a detriment.", "token_estimate": 204, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:30:02", "end_timestamp": "00:30:02", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "0eff652d-8a54-4d7f-a327-d537e9144d9c", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Darren Josey (00:30:53): Yeah, it's certainly a delicate balance and it runs pretty counter to what I think a lot of folks in the outdoor recreation just for their own personal use, no one wants to see, or most people don't want to see more people at their local surf spot or their local crag. And I've definitely experienced that too. I love for me, well now I do more bouldering as a dad, that's what I have time for. But pre my beautiful daughter, my wife and I spent a lot of time sport climbing at Rumney and parking Lot Wall would be packed, and I'm sure it's been even more packed post pandemic, especially when gyms got closed down. But if we aren't talking about the other amazing climbing areas that are 15 minute hike in a different direction, then we're never going to spread out. But at the same time, we want to make sure we make good stewards of anyone else trying new areas, stay on the trails, really use leave no trace. (00:31:51): Even though leave no trace, folks on their site have kind of changed their stance on geo tagging. So we don't want to be reckless as a brand and say, Hey, here's anywhere that you can go. If we're even going to call an area that could be more ecologically fragile, let's call it out for that and say, Hey, this area needs more resources and work with land management company to get people to either donate time or money to help that area thrive. Because if we don't all spread out, then we're going to lose or not make the most of this moment in time for a lot more people recreating the place. The reason why we as a brand want to do this is that climate change is the crisis of our lifetime, and we have to make real reductions in the carbon that we put out there from producing products and transporting our products. (00:32:45): So that's on us, the brand, but we also want to take this moment to create more stewards of our lands because we really believe that the more people have a good time outside, the more people are going to vote to keep these areas open and accessible. More people are going to spend the time to keep these areas clean and safe and then teach their kids this is how to go into the outdoors. And we don't really want to define what outdoor recreation is, but we do want to show more people the possibilities because I think overwhelmingly people want to experience this in kind of, I guess the word is you don't want to be a Jerry, right? No one wants to go out and kind of fumble around and not know how to get out there. But at the same time, we want to make sure people express themselves as they want to out there. So I hope we're doing it. I mean, we still have a long way to go. It's certainly not perfect, and I really welcome a lot of feedback from others on how we can add more resources and provide more opportunities for people to have a good time and a safe time outside.", "token_estimate": 716, "speakers": ["Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:30:53", "end_timestamp": "00:30:53", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "72eb8263-c061-4103-8b17-bd03d3bdeb31", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:33:51): So I know recently you guys have been going through a website, refresh, overhaul, whatever you want to call it, and I think some of these values that we've been talking about today are being reflected in this new website and it sounds like the old website was very sales focused, whereas this new refresh is very, well, I'll let, how about you describe it? What's been the project with the website and what does it look like today?\n\nDarren Josey (00:34:19): Sure. So we just launched it at the start of March. It was a long time coming. We actually, it's pretty crazy because we launched it in about, let's see, I hired my digital director in October and we launched it in March. So pretty quick. It was something that I had been conceptualizing since 2020, but we really wanted to, I looked at a lot of other consumer good sites and when you land on those sites, it's Shop now, buy now, explore these products, pop up in your face, say 15%. We just didn't want to be that brand. And I actually really love the way, I believe it was Al Perkinson said it on your podcast, if you want to be part of the community, you got to contribute to it first. I think he kept saying, buy the beer, pay the band. That's what we wanted to do with our site. (00:35:06): I loved hearing him say it. I was like, that's all we're trying to do with the adventure resources. We want to give something to people so they could have a better time recreating as a way to say, Hey, we're here as a resource. We want you to keep coming back. And so when you hit our homepage, you're going to see only about half the stories or anything to do with buying a product. A lot of it is our sustainability, all sorts of different things from places to recreate and skills to learn. And we really wanted to, I'm excited for next month we have a couple more drops we're going to be doing on expanding out our sustainability page with a really awesome timeline of past, present, and future and what we're doing on sustainability, but also are about us because NEMO is, I think becoming rarer and rarer that a company of our size is majority family owned. (00:35:58): Cam is the CEO, the founder and majority owner, and he's a designer. So NEMO as a brand is product led and values driven. And we really want to make sure we have a website that communicates that because we're, we're a seasonal business and we want someone to be thinking positively about our brand when they pull out their camping gear at the start of the season and to go get timely emails that drive you back to the site to say, Hey, get your gear ready. Here's how to repair something. Here's how to clean it. Here's how to store it. Here's some training to get your body ready. Here's some great locations that you may not have thought of. Here's some events that are going to be coming up in your area, all driving traffic back to a site that is designed to be a resource to people and not so much a convert here. (00:36:47): I think Nemo very wisely to the time to not focus on converting on our website. We are a wholesale first brand, and I feel so fortunate to lead a marketing team thanks to very clean distribution that already has all the a and b doors in town and doesn't, I'm not pressured to get people to convert on our website. It's more how can we have people come back and learn something and then let's drive traffic to the retailer if they happen to convert on our site. Great. As our head of sales, Mike says, our website will always be the most expensive place to find our products and we we're proud of that. I think we're going to see in 2022 a renaissance of brick and mortar sales. I mean, every single year e-commerce continues to go up, but I think we maybe in the outdoor industry, forget that there's a lot of people in this country that see shopping as a sport, that people love to go out there, go to stores, browse. (00:37:50): And I think in particular, if you sell expensive goods, ones that really have to do with, I want to touch it, I want to feel it. I want to see the space of it. I mean the Stargaze luxury client or chair, it's hard to understand how awesome that product is, even with the most phenomenal product page to sit auto recline and rock in it. You have to go to a store to experience that. And that's why a lot of our retailers put that thing right out front. People walk up and go, what's that? Sit down and go, oh yeah, I want one of these. And that's what we want. So to continue to drive our traffic to retail stores to buy stuff, but have that website where we keep providing new resources and another reason to come back, that's why we built it and how we built it.", "token_estimate": 1171, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:33:51", "end_timestamp": "00:34:19", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "5ab556e3-13d0-448d-a3f5-6e3c9049bcc4", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:38:34): So at the very beginning of this podcast, we were talking about the things that the outdoor industry does well and some of the things that maybe we're behind on compared to other industries. This idea of creating a non-transactional relationship, do you feel like as a whole this is, that's something that the industry, how do we perform? Do we do a good job? Do we do a poor job?\n\nDarren Josey (00:38:58): I think we're getting better. There's certainly, I'm seeing more representation in marketing materials and I think some brands do have things built further back on their website that you can really dig to find out. But I think we could be doing a lot better job in making more of these resources, connecting people to others if we have an honest understanding of what we're selling. No one has to do these things. You don't have to go camping, you don't have to go skiing. I think we feel that sometimes I haven't surfed in a month and a half, and I'm right at that point where I really need to get in the water, but I don't have to. And I have other priorities in my life that can come forward and have not allowed me to do that. And if we maintain that perspective that the things that we're selling no one has to do, I think it changes why. (00:39:52): And when we talk to our customer, I mean, I've definitely been part of other friend groups where we're only one friend away. If that person moves away, we're not going to do that trip anymore. And then who's going to bring people together? And then that sort of just crumbles. And I think we have a lot of groups like that in our society where they're one family away if they move, it's kind of done. Or that person was like, yeah, I just had kids and I don't want to prioritize this anymore. I can't do this. And then no one's there to pick up the slack. So I think to one, take advantage of this moment in time where we have so many new people who've come in and to really make sure that, and not take for granted our core customers who've been doing this for a bit. (00:40:34): I mean, we have a really huge opportunity with the boomer generation of a lot of folks who have retired in the last couple of years and they are recreating. And that's actually why, or one of the key reasons why we wanted to show some older folks too in our marketing materials because they're looking to get outside as well. I mean, I definitely know a lot of my own family and friends and their parents who started to recreate outside. I was actually giving a talk at my high school for Martin Luther King Day, and one of the other panelists was an older black gentleman who had bought a, he and his wife bought their first camping setup ever, and they're in their late sixties and he was like, I'm just not sure there's a safe space for me to go. Where should I go camping? And I'm like, ah, see, this is what he needs. (00:41:25): He needs to see someone who looks like himself as well as some other materials to get him outside. He went to a store, he bought the, and then was like, okay, now where do I go? And I think we as an industry need to create more materials for him and his wife. They're looking to get outside and he had basically sat on that camping gear for a year. He didn't know where to go. And I think that's one big area that we can make some pretty quick improvements there for both. I think we spent a lot of time talking about Gen Z, which we should, right? They're the next big age group to come up and start making more purchasing decisions. But let's also not forget about the boomer generation as well as our Gen X folks who maybe grew up with a different interpretation of outdoor adventures. (00:42:15): And we really want to be inclusive. I mean, I've been on a lot of calls where a brand or a group can pivot hard in another direction, and we're nimo really trying to find this balance of we really want to be inclusive. And that's understanding. Some groups need different motivations, different resources, and other groups like our core group, they should still feel like this is a brand they recognize. We don't want to make a hard pivot and have someone who's been an ultralight through hiker with us for a decade and hit our homepage and go, what's this? I don't know this brand. We've certainly seen brands do that over the years, and we really want to truly be inclusive to our core audience as well as newcomers that if you're brand new or you're experience, you hit that homepage and you go, this is the brand I grew up with, or this is the brand that is welcoming to me and my changing taste or my first time, this is welcoming to beginners.", "token_estimate": 1164, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:38:34", "end_timestamp": "00:38:58", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "cf122386-afc5-4af6-ab9f-26990036976c", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:43:18): So how do you do that?\n\nDarren Josey (00:43:21): We put it down to pure numbers. We put out content ratios for our activities. We're still a core backpacking brand and about 25% of the stories we want to always have on our homepage that showcase backpacking. And we want to be more like 15% around car camping. We're still pretty new in the car camping space, but we also want to continue to show about five to 7% of mountaineering. We still want to embrace those epic images from someone on the top of a glaciated peak out there solo doing an incredible trip that's still this brand. We still make great gear for that. We also want to show some of the other activities. I mean, we did a partnership with Firstlight in the hunt and fish space. We still we're actually building out a landing page to talk more about that partnership later this year. But that's something where in the future, we really want to be your home for adventure. (00:44:20): We don't just want to be, or lemme say this, we have an opportunity to bring together the hunt fish crowd and the core outdoor crowd. I think a lot of younger consumers don't necessarily see themselves in those buckets. I'm also an angler. I've never gone hunting in my life, but I love fishing. I don't necessarily consider myself hook and bullet. I guess I consider myself more core, but I do all of those things. I'm equally as excited to take my daughter fishing for the first time as I am to go with my cousin and rent a charter boat and go striper fishing as I am to get back out and lead an awesome sunny sport climb. And I think there's a real opportunity there to bring those two groups together and that's why we want to keep showing some representation of that. So that hardcore outdoor, oh yeah, that's part of this brand as well. And there's other opportunities here that we could all just share this land together because man, when we do that, when we bring those industries together, look out, I think we're really going to be a force for land conservation as well as making sure this plant's around for many more generations to come.", "token_estimate": 531, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:43:18", "end_timestamp": "00:43:21", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "fbcb56ed-f61a-4776-b062-f925d631cec1", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:45:33): Yeah. So something that we talked a little bit about was you said you have three main models or three main ambassadors on your homepage, and I love this idea of everything that we've been talking about has been kind of grounded in the homepage and it seems like it is the central location for all of these topics and values being represented. And I know that you guys went through a pretty extensive process trying to find those people, vet them, make sure they're the right people. But something that we didn't talk about that I know is also important for you guys is compensation and paying models for their time and what they're providing to the brand. So that's a conversation that I see photographers and filmmakers and producers talking about. But I'm curious from the brand side, how do you guys think through that?", "token_estimate": 212, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:45:33", "end_timestamp": "00:45:33", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "16fe2691-a574-4a0c-b0e9-e52d5958ec24", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Darren Josey (00:46:22): Sure. I definitely follow quite a few underrepresented and marginalized folks in the outdoor industry on social media. And one meme in particular that I saw someone share was, I can't pay my bills with representation. And that was really important I think to call out that there were brands that were trying to take advantage of, Hey, if I just put you in this ad, your face is going to be seen and that's going to help you. I'm not going to pay you, or maybe I'll give you some gear and that's it. That's just not fair. I kind of joke, but not joke when you're talking about DEI, that E is capitalized and honestly as at least five points want bigger than the diversity and inclusion, right? There is no diversity inclusion without the equity. So when we were putting together our brand shoot last year to be shown this year, the first thing I wanted to do was make sure we found some really good folks that were of underrepresented or marginalized communities. (00:47:26): The second was to make sure that all of them either owned or worked for a company that built pathways to adventure because what we were trying to do was first pay our model's top modeling rate. So I asked a few other folks who were professional photographers in our industry and in others to try to figure out the right model day rate, and we covered all expenses, so transportation, food, lodging, and then we made sure that as part of the contract along with the production company, we have three year rights on that, that the model's rights were also would require to be renewed. So if we want to show them in new materials after three years, just like we'd negotiate with the production company, we would renegotiate with them. And then we want to go to what I was calling exponential equity. So each model has a spot on our adventure resources page where we have a link that we spend our marketing dollars and resources driving our traffic to click to hire them, right? (00:48:28): Because I want to really go beyond, we just cut a check and said, Hey, thanks for your likeness. We're going to throw it on all sorts of stuff and then see you later. We're done with you. We wanted to really make sure that we showcased their stories and provided that opportunity for our fans and followers to interact and engage with them. And then just on a, I'd say a more savvy marketing side, all three of those folks. So our three models for this year were Mario Stanley, who was a rock climbing coach and a guide out of Texas who's actually on an HBO reality climbing series, which I'm really excited to see when that comes out that Chris Sharma and Jason Mao put together. So go Mario. I hope he did well on that. Loosely Tuma, who I mentioned a little earlier is a co-founder of Latinx hikers and then Lori Watt, who is a guide of rock climbing, ice climbing, mountaineering and backpacking who serves in both the Pacific Northwest and New England. (00:49:31): And so really between those three folks, we really want to provide those resources to, so we can email folks like for Lori, we can email our people in the Pacific Northwest and New England about events she has going on or an inspirational video to see. Oh wow, that's cool. That's Lori, she's in my neighborhood. And Latinx hikers does stuff throughout the country, so we're really excited to be sending traffic to different events that are happening there. And then Maro does stuff mostly in the south in Texas and the Texas Mexico border. So to find other areas. And then I'm really super excited for Tristan Hogue, who's our fourth marketer who could not come down to the US because of COVID. He's in Canada, he's building out a guiding business, and once his guiding site is up, I'll be really excited to add his link and image to that adventure resources page. (00:50:21): So folks who are looking to do some pretty crazy backpacking and ultralight, he did an amazing adventure last year called The Angels Operation, where he tried to do 32 Canadian peaks in 35 days solo. Yeah, pretty awesome stuff. So I think to have a nice inclusive inspirational group so people can see different types of pathways to adventure. The other part we did for Mario Lu and Laurie, each of them had a different pathway to adventure, and we did short documentaries on each because those folks, Mario didn't think outdoor recreation was something that he did. No one said it. He didn't see anyone else doing it who looked like him. Mario is African-American. He didn't see other people that looked like him doing it until someone's like, Hey, why don't you try this? He didn't know it existed. Lucy's story, she went hiking on a trip with her brother and was like, wow, this is really amazing. (00:51:27): She did it in SCO, Peru, and she did it and came back home and then used social media to find other areas to go and to meet up with other folks. And I think that story is really lost. I think there is some probably generational chafing there on older folks or maybe some more core outdoor enthusiasts that are like, oh, technology in the outdoors, get out of here with that crap. Like cell phone towers in our national parks. Oh, what a tragedy. But if you reframe it as someone who didn't have friends and family who were readily interested to go on this, someone who found their community through social media, someone who came from a first generation immigrant story. So her parents immigrated to the United States and don't see the value in what she does for fun and recreation. In fact, they see it as a negative. (00:52:23): Why would you waste your time to go drive a couple hours to the mountains and go walk around? Which is why when she summits a peak, one of the first things she does is tries to, she FaceTimes her parents, she wants to show them, look at what this is like. I want you to experience this with me. And she has been able to do that. She has actually been able to take them, but she talks about in her short doc hearing and feeling the negativity out there of other people going, look at that girl on her phone. Can you believe this girl on her cell phone while she's hiking? Oh my God, put yourself in that person's shoes. She has to her own community saying, we don't do this. Why would you do this? Don't do this. And it's not that the outdoors is telling you don't do it. (00:53:11): In some cases you have your own community saying, don't do this. So she's trying to convince other people just, Hey, this is an opportunity. I'm trying to leverage the technology to inspire more folks, even my own family to come do this with me. Then Lori's story, pretty awesome. So Lori became a guide actually in her early fifties, and she's someone who started out recreating very early in her life as a little kid, and through I think a lot of the normal stuff that anyone goes through, career, family built out this awesome career as a physical therapist and sort of gotten away from her adventure story. And when her kids went off to college, she was like, you know what? Lemme get back to this. And hired guides and had a great time outside and then just felt she had this calling and asked one of the guides, is there space for me to become a guide? (00:54:00): And they were like, yeah, come on in. Let's get you trained up. If you want to do this, let's do this. And that's what she did. So she's now a full-time guide. She actually shut down her PT business and guides full-time, and she's someone who's absolutely amazing. I got to do a charity backpacking trip with her four or five years ago, and I said, Lori, someday we're going to work together. And I'm so thrilled to say that we actually got to work together on this brand campaign. So we call out in our market materials, all of those folks by name. We have those links. We really want to, if all of their businesses are thriving, that means Nemo's business is going to be thriving because people have more pathways to adventure.", "token_estimate": 1991, "speakers": ["Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:46:22", "end_timestamp": "00:46:22", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "b70019f7-d9c1-47cc-ab6f-24abed3d8c9a", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:54:42): Yeah. Well, Darren, I think it's listening to you share some of these stories. It's obvious that there is no shortage of passion from yourself personally and obviously from what the whole team at NEMO is doing. And I think what's really neat is hearing how a brand such as NEMO can fill that gap. If people don't have a role model or someone to ask questions of, and I think all this kind of ladders up to this idea of you guys are trying to be that friend, be that guide, and in doing so, getting people outside, but also building ways, building relationship with people, not just through selling your product, which I think is pretty cool to hear about and listen to the journey that you guys are on. We are unfortunately coming close to the end of our time for today, but do you have any other thoughts or anything else you want to leave the audience with today?", "token_estimate": 223, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:54:42", "end_timestamp": "00:54:42", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "8bf0b1c3-f514-4ff4-9d74-bf673f4a2aee", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Darren Josey (00:55:35): Yeah, I would say we as an industry probably need to take some more risks on finding underrepresented voices to move further up the corporate ranks. I think in my own career, I'm definitely someone who has been trying to keep moving up and I feel like I've finally found a great home as well as a great platform to help evangelize more people of more communities to get outside for a lifetime. But it's definitely a little lonely. There aren't many black and brown VPs of anything in corporate America, and if I'm not doing my part to at least prove that we can earn a spot, a seat at the table, then I shouldn't be surprised when there's no one else in the room with me. So I really want to encourage other folks. I mean, I was, two years ago my title said manager. Now I was managing a couple million dollars in eight people, but it was quite the struggle to find a place that would give me that opportunity. (00:56:49): I'm thankful for all the opportunities I've had, but I want to make sure there's opportunities for other people and we at NEMO are trying to hire people from outside of our industry and hire people that maybe their title doesn't exactly say what it is, everything that they're doing, and I really hope that we as an industry can continue or try harder to find people from outside the industry as well as give shots to people who may not have the title that we think we want to hire for. Because a lot of the experience, if we're only looking to hire the people that already have that title, then we're just going to keep hiring the same couple of people.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:57:30): Yeah. I think folks could probably come up with an answer for this just listening to you, but I'm curious, where does your passion and your excitement come from all of this?", "token_estimate": 451, "speakers": ["Darren Josey", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:55:35", "end_timestamp": "00:57:30", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "bf6b0ec7-9af6-4b2e-ab3b-626c5bf7765d", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Darren Josey (00:57:42): Man, I don't think I've ever really questioned that. I'm someone who's certainly, I love fun. I probably put fun is my number one. I think we had to go through this leadership exercise and name the two things you care most about and fun and understanding were the two things that I really had. I just love to have fun and I'm also willing to put in the work to have fun because a lot of fun. I found this as my career has gotten more intense as my life, as other priorities have come up. Being a parent and being a husband, trying to be really great at both of those is hard and fun can certainly take a backseat sometimes, but I think the work, putting in the work to have fun is a great amount of fulfillment and understanding other people's perspectives really helps me focus on what is important in life and to think about things differently. (00:58:42): I constantly want to learn, and it's funny as I've gotten older, I'm going to be 38 this year. I know now so much of what I don't know. I think you asked me when I was 22, I legitimately thought I knew almost everything, and now I know so much more than I did then. But I also know, wow, I have so much more to learn and that excites me. It can be a little scary at times because especially sitting with this title, there's more of an expectation that, wow, you already know everything. But again, I'll speak to the appraises of Cam as the leader of this company. He's made it a very welcoming and encourages people to tell others when they don't know and to really listen. I think that's something where early in my career I spent too much time talking, especially someone who now I know did not know that much as I do now, but I really tried to spend more time and listening and listening because there's so many different perspectives out there, and the better job I can do of taking those perspectives and applying my own learning the better and honestly happier I'm going to be. (00:59:52): Going back to that understanding, I increase my understanding. I'm also going to increase the fun that I have, and I feel like I can do a better job in my career.", "token_estimate": 536, "speakers": ["Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "00:57:42", "end_timestamp": "00:57:42", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "a38fd4a9-0ef2-477a-af26-8c5985becc0c", "episode_id": "2fee4450-9da7-496a-aa58-56a40a2fd74a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488a", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:00:01): Yeah. Awesome, man. Well, I appreciate you sharing. This has been fun.\n\nDarren Josey (01:00:06): That's been a blast, man. I appreciate the resource. Keep talking about resources. I really see this podcast as a great resource for someone who, I mean marketing is changing every single day, so I appreciate you putting it on, and I'm excited to keep listening.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:00:22): Well, thank you. If folks want to follow along with you or with nemo, where can they go to connect?\n\nDarren Josey (01:00:28): LinkedIn's probably best, right? Darren, Josie. I'm up there and you'll see me kind of in the background of some of our marketing materials. We are still a small company, so yeah, we hired three models that are also outdoor enthusiasts, but NEMO has a long tradition of putting the staff in shoots because, well, we're already being paid by the company, but two, it's a lot easier to run a marketing shoot where you could be the PA as well as the producer, as well as the talent.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:00:55): Yeah, you got to love those types of shoots. Yeah, you're wearing all the hats.\n\nDarren Josey (01:01:00): Yeah, we always do a dedicated marketing team shoot too, so I'm excited to plan that with the rest of the team this year too.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:01:07): Very cool. Well, I look forward to seeing some of the initiatives that you guys are working on come rolling out in the next month or so. You've mentioned a few different projects that are in the works, so definitely be looking forward to those. But in the meantime, I hope you find time to get out and get into the waves and fulfill your surf. But until then, yeah, it's great to meet you and I appreciate you taking the time to share your story with us.\n\nDarren Josey (01:01:29): Thanks for your time and for the platform, Cole. Really appreciate what you do and the opportunity.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:01:35): Thank you. Alright, well have a great rest of your day. You too. See you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learn something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 549, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Darren Josey"], "start_timestamp": "01:00:01", "end_timestamp": "01:01:35", "podcast_index": 100, "episode_name": "Ep. 100: Building a Non-Transactional Relationship With Your Customer | Darren Josey | NEMO Equipment", "slug": "ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-100-building-a-non-transactional-relationship-with-your-customer-darren-josey-nemo-equipment", "guest_name": "Darren Josey", "guest_title": "VP of Marketing at NEMO", "categories": [], "short_description": "Darren sits down with Cole to discuss how to build a non-transactional relationship with your customer, creating better customer relationships at large.", "podcast_length": "1:02:00"}, {"id": "be9209dd-7a93-4f29-80b2-a089c90eabd4", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Megan Averell (00:00:02): I mean, we are in sort of that business of resonance. If you are saying stuff, it doesn't matter how factual it is. It doesn't matter if you're 20% better than your competitor. What matters is, is it resonant with them personally? Does it compel them to act? And of course, what we know and what I know as a researcher is that apathy is our biggest motivation. You're having to break through people's biggest motivation in order to get to those next level sets of motivations if you think about it, to get them to change behavior, to try your product or service.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Holborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo, and today I'm sitting down with Megan Avril. She's the founder of the Insight in Megan. How are you?\n\nMegan Averell (00:01:15): I'm great. Thank you so much for having me.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:16): Thank you. I appreciate you taking the time out of your day. How's your Monday going?\n\nMegan Averell (00:01:22): Not too bad, actually. I was very excited to share with you that I did some roller skating last night after probably 20 odd years of not roller skating, which was delightful. So highly recommend that to any listener who wants to have a good time On a random night,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:37): Do you feel like roller skating will make a comeback or is it already making a comeback that we're not aware of?", "token_estimate": 493, "speakers": ["Megan Averell", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00:02", "end_timestamp": "00:01:37", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "701f9995-b1a7-4736-be0e-82e5a74e2541", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Megan Averell (00:01:44): There was a heck of a lot of sales of roller skates and roller blades during the pandemic, and they couldn't keep up with demand, so they were sold out of stock in both adult and kids sizes. And so a friend of mine had brought their own last night and showed off their pink patent leather roller skates that somehow in the middle of that huge surge in the pandemic they were able to find. So yeah, it's a personal mobility thing that you can do on your own to stay fit. And so during the pandemic, I think it went a little nuts. So it's a question of whether they'll sustain their comeback. I think\n\nCole Heilborn (00:02:20): We hear about that in the bike industry and the water sports and kayak and sub industry. I've never heard that though from the roller blade industry that they also experience all the same problems.\n\nMegan Averell (00:02:31): There was a really fascinating jump where people were going into the materials that were used, right, due to the supply chain issues, to manufacture some of these things. And just how, because no one could anticipate this time where we were all going to be very isolated and alone and looking for ways to work out the roller skates and roller blades only had so much stock and only so much that they were preparing for, and especially the adult right market went a little bananas because I think we think of roller skating as something, as I was talking to my friends the other night that only kids do or you only do when you're a kid, but there was a whole resurgence around adults and rediscovering that. It was lovely.", "token_estimate": 400, "speakers": ["Megan Averell", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:01:44", "end_timestamp": "00:02:31", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "07ee229c-bd3a-4fc8-ba87-641bebad1c14", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:03:13): Well, I'm going to have to look into that now. Maybe I'll pick that up as another hobby. Well, Megan, first I want to say I love the name of your business, the insight in. I have lots of questions and a lot of curiosity around the name. Fortunately, we've had the chance to connect prior to this call, and so I'm really excited to bring you out of the show and talk about it. Topic today that we've never discussed research and the intricacies and the fun, the joy, maybe some of the challenges of good research in all things, but specifically in marketing. So Megan, I'd love if you could just give us a quick little introduction about yourself, where you're located, what kind of your background, and what you do.", "token_estimate": 186, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03:13", "end_timestamp": "00:03:13", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "9c201821-4bd1-4cf7-a0cd-5a45b7d9224f", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Megan Averell (00:03:53): Yeah, so it's funny that you mentioned that about my company name. It feels a little old school to mention this, but I actually originally when I was starting my qualitative business and a little bit before that, I was really loved staying at Airbnbs, not Airbnb. I do stay at Airbnb, but bed and breakfast for a long time because it was a real clear way to meet people that came from all different walks of life. Usually there's a breakfast for all of the Gen Zers on the call who may not know what a better breakfast is. Usually you're hosted by people who own some kind of home. A lot of times it happened to be a historic home, and then they would cook breakfast and they would only do it one time and at a certain time, and there was a limited number of guests, so never more than 12 or 15 guests, and that would be kind of a boom day, right? (00:04:43): So you kind of have this intimate breakfast setting with some totally random people you've never met before, and the conversation of just naturally, oh, what are you doing in town? What are you here for? You would learn about new things. You would hear about different people's life experiences, and there wasn't a lot of opportunities in life other than in social media to sit down and have a face-to-face mail with multiple strangers you'd never met and who had nothing ostensibly in common other than where they were. And so I loved the idea of that kind of cross section and that cross conversation coming into my research business. So when I founded the insight in it was partly because of that and also partly because a double entendre there with bringing insight back into business. Because I do think, and I'm thinking that your podcast listeners probably feel the same, that word insight has gotten a little bit overused to the point of extinction. (00:05:40): I think it's not very meaningful when we toss it around all the time for anything as bland as people love their families and spending time with their families, well, that's not an insight, but with the misuse of it over time, I think people kind of go, oh, the insight, well, that's not going to be very good. So the goal of course, of any good research and consultancy and strategy business is to bring good actionable, real insights forward and to bring that back into business. So that's the other double entendre with the name. So my background, I was an advertising strategist for 10 years on the agency side. I started my career at McCann Erickson in New York and DDB in Chicago and all these wonderful places, learning from really smart strategic leaders in the business. And then made my way out to the best coast where I am still today. (00:06:31): And after about a decade in advertising and an ad strategy particularly, which was always about representing the consumer, that particular role for anyone who's not as familiar with agency side, you're completely laser focused on the consumer. What are they thinking? What are they feeling? What are they going to do? And you kind of represent that person in the process, whereas the creative director is representing the art and the account person is representing what the client's point of view is. You kind of come in and fight for that consumer. And surprisingly, that role involved very little consumer interaction, which was a little bit frustrating. We weren't often talking to consumers as much as I wished, even though we were often trying to glean what we could to represent their thinking and their way of being. So after that 10 year stint and advertising was like, I want to get closer to their thoughts and opinions and their lives and really get in there. (00:07:26): And that's when I switched into doing my own thing and qualitative research. I had a brief stint in Australia, which was really fun, where I actually worked for a qualitative research and consultancy firm to kind of get the nuts and bolts of the business down before I came back and founded the insight in. So I've been doing this for seven years now on my own. And I work for companies that are big and small, everybody from startups to fortune fives and really across categories, which I have some experience in the outdoor realm with some clients like outdoor research and a couple others. And then also everything from sort of car repair to smoothies to coffee, to you name it. So I get to see people across so many aspects of their life, and I think that's a really lovely thing too, because we often can get quite specialized, and that is so good to have that deep vertical knowledge. But it's nice to have somebody like me come in and say, ah, yes, and this reminds me of how people make decisions in finance, and people will be like, what? That's interesting. So it affords me that opportunity to really understand them through all walks of their life.", "token_estimate": 1215, "speakers": ["Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:03:53", "end_timestamp": "00:03:53", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "a8890bf5-f45d-4dcf-a1e7-dba07834f40f", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:08:30): Yeah. Well, you bring up a great question. Let's define in your words, what is an insight? How do you know when you've stumbled across a good insight?\n\nMegan Averell (00:08:40): Yeah, there's so many definitions out there, which is fantastic, and I think everybody has their own, I mean mine, it just comes back to that idea of just to some untold truth. So something that you absolutely positively feel as soon as somebody mentions it or as soon as you hear it out loud, but you may not have put your finger on prior. It's not everybody enjoys spending time with their family. Everybody enjoys spending time with their family except when, and then there's some twist to it. So there's always just a little bit of truth that exists somewhere in your soul or in your heart, and as soon as somebody mentioned it, you go, yeah, you know what? I've always felt that way, or Yeah, I totally feel that way. So I think for me, I don't have a beautiful articulation of it as much as I want that moment where when I say that and I am reading a brief out loud or I'm talking to folks about the way consumers feel or have felt, and people go, oh yeah, I actually totally get that. I am feeling that somewhere in my body or I'm feeling that viscerally. I'm like, yeah, then that was super successful. So I think it's the reaction to it too of that kind of, I knew that, but I didn't know. I knew that I thought about that, but didn't know I thought that way. And that's a really nice moment to be in if you happen to share some affinity to your target group. Otherwise, you may not be able to have that yourself. But yeah, that's a nice moment.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:10:09): Okay, interesting. Yeah, I like what you said, it's like this visceral realization where you're like, oh, I didn't understand that, or I didn't realize that on a much deeper level.", "token_estimate": 467, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:08:30", "end_timestamp": "00:10:09", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "f6b2b7a7-9be4-441f-b21a-909716a72483", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Megan Averell (00:10:21): And I think we're often pressed for time in marketing. Everything is due yesterday. And so I certainly don't cast any blame for times that you kind of have to go with a truth that's just really well known. But yeah, I think there's what I'd hope for and what I think a lot of strategy people and marketing people hope for is that next level down of, okay, and part of that leads into research. I really feel like research is asking the question before the question very often. So if it's like, I want people to activate and do what I want them to do, I need to back out of that and ask the question before that of like, well, what's pushing on them and motivating them? What's making them feel that way? What's driving them? Oh, it's this 20-year-old perception of my category. Oh no, why did that stick with them? (00:11:09): What is it about that that really bugs them? Oh, there's probably an emotion there. Is that feeling like guilt or shame or inadequacy? And then, okay, well, why would someone be feeling that? Well, there's probably a cultural element that's pushing down on them that's telling them that they should feel that way when they get into a situation. So how do we understand at a cultural level what's happening? And so you're really just kind of taking what is and what on the face, and then you're sort of backing multiple questions out of that that involve further wise. And that to me is a really good way to get to an insight. Say you start with something that everybody knows, well then start to interrogate that. And when you go multiple levels deeper, and maybe if you're able to engage with a researcher or you're just able to answer those questions to the best of your ability, I do think you can get to that question before the question kind of format can get you to something real or something deeper or something another level down than just, oh, the behavior we witness is that five out of 10 people enjoy citrus. (00:12:11): It's like, yeah, okay, we know that that's a known truth, so how do we get to why that might be the case? And then how do we get to something twisty within that?", "token_estimate": 537, "speakers": ["Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:10:21", "end_timestamp": "00:10:21", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "8297ce4e-5d7e-4a33-aa28-07df943842cc", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:12:18): Is that an actual statistic that five out of 10 people, it's\n\nMegan Averell (00:12:21): Not? Oh,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:12:21): Okay. Yeah. Very interesting. Are there, I don't know if this is a thing, but are there any famous research studies or cases or pieces of data that everyone would probably be familiar with, whether consciously or subconsciously, is there anything out there in the history of research that people can probably relate to in some way?", "token_estimate": 121, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:12:18", "end_timestamp": "00:12:21", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "2b1c3ca4-3c1f-401f-816f-2a480f1df655", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Megan Averell (00:12:45): I think one of the great intersections of research and marketing is the story of the original planner at TBW Shi a day, whose name is now escaping me unfortunately. But the original got mail campaign, which many marketers, at least over a certain age are familiar with. That was really about that moment that you realize that you don't have milk and then you freak out, you need it, right? You're having a giant chocolate chip cookie or whatever it is. And how they got to that campaign was actually through research, because as you might imagine, milk was something that was at that time before the advent of almond milk and before the advent of oat milk and some of those things, it was in everybody's fridge, but no one had a particular affinity towards it. It was hard to articulate what that affinity was. And what I really like about the study that they concocted for that was that it required what I like to call research design, which is you don't just get in there and ask questions. (00:13:46): That's not, I mean, you can, and that's certainly better than nothing, but it doesn't actually get to like, oh, what are we actually trying to find? And then let me backtrack from there and design to get there. And so in this case, it was a very specific problem, which is that upwards of 90% of Americans had milk in the refrigerator at any given time, but no one really gave a crap, right? It was just sort of there. It was very banal. And so if you had loaded a bunch of people into a focus group and said, what do you like about milk? They would've been like, I don't know. It's there. It's kind of creamy, it's kind of good. It's cooling. Sometimes it just would've been a very fruitless conversation. And they realized that. So their research approach was to take it away and they just basically wouldn't allow people to buy milk. (00:14:33): And there's a study named for this, and it's called deprivation study. And sometimes when there's consumer goods or products that we use all the time, but it's very hard for us to put our finger on what it is about that we care about or why that brand might be different than just X brand or a generic brand or things because it's been a part of our life that actually if you actually remove and take that brand out of people's lives, they may have something new that they stumble on as to why that matters to them. And so sure enough, the actual anecdotes that they used inside the study of eating a bunch of Gabi peanut butter or eating a big chocolate chip cookie and just feeling like that experience wasn't quite what it could be if you had milk, all of those. And then of course there was a very iconic moment where they flipped in the ad, the jug upside down because they were either out or it wasn't in the fridge and it was like got milk. (00:15:26): That was the pin on that campaign. But almost every single one of those scenarios, even though they were made up creatively and ended up looking really cool and had some real creative pizazz to them, they really did come from things. People actually said, oh my gosh, I can't believe I can't have milk with this. I didn't realize what it meant to me. So that's a great example. And I actually have done deprivation studies in my career as well, because sometimes, and we've all can think of some off the top of our head, there are goods and services that are just there, and maybe we use them at a convenience and maybe that's all we could say about them. If you went and asked someone and tapped 'em on the shoulder, oh, well, it's right on the corner. I use it all the time. But if you are able to put people in an artificial situation through research design where they don't have access to that anymore, suddenly they're able to articulate what it is that's really going on, what's really attracting them or repelling them from other brands even, which can get you to differentiation. (00:16:22): So it was a famous example, and I wish I remembered the name of the planner, but it was a really fantastic case study and it's one that's often cited.", "token_estimate": 1023, "speakers": ["Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:12:45", "end_timestamp": "00:12:45", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "c526384a-2774-41f1-8f04-638a41ab0864", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:16:31): Yeah, that's really fascinating. So what would that process look like? Would they bring people into a room and then feed them a cookie but not give them milk? Or how are they reaching this conclusion?\n\nMegan Averell (00:16:44): So they basically just said, okay, you're going to participate in this study and as part of your run of household, you cannot buy milk. You can go to the grocery store and buy everything else, but you can't buy milk. And then they had 'em, I think, keep a journal, and that was back then would've been maybe a physical journal. But now of course we can do those kinds of studies where we're just kind of not necessarily pulling people out of their lives, but we're more like they're touching base with us within their lives. They might log on to now something like a little online discussion board and be like, Hey, here's how I'm feeling on day three of no milk. So without actually, and I think they did some focus groups and things like that, but you're essentially saying just keep living your life and just keep doing what you're doing, but we are going to have this one artificial variable, and then you're going to check in with us about how that is going. (00:17:34): How is that affecting you, if at all? Because sometimes you can't guarantee that it will. I mean, maybe they'll be like, Hey, I found something much better in the interim, and you're like, oh, no. But you kind of have to be open to that journey to find out. But yeah, those are the kinds of studies where you really get into people's lives and you're more flittering above like a drone every once in a while to come in and capture their feedback. But those can be quite longitudinal as well. Sometimes you might do a study like that if it was, for instance, I wanted to know how people buy a car. Well, that's a long research process that they might go through. So I might fl into their lives once a month and check in with them and be like, Hey, how's it going? What's your process right now? What have been some of the twists and turns since we talked last? And you can get a sense of something that's quite a long journey without having to lock people up for 12 months in a focus group facility.", "token_estimate": 557, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:16:31", "end_timestamp": "00:16:44", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "47015ba8-ecf8-4c21-9d82-d803bdff6752", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:18:27): So I'm curious to learn more about your process because we're talking about how these different studies come to be, and there's obvious that there's a lot of work that goes into the preparation and planning of these things. I guess can you explain that? And as a researcher, what is your goal or what are you working towards?\n\nMegan Averell (00:18:47): Yeah, I think that I started my own, this dovetails really well into why I started my own research firm, which is that I think there isn't a lot of care given to research design specifically. I think it's much easier to churn out a list of questions to just sit down and brainstorm and be like, okay, here's our list of 50 questions, and we'll hire sort of a moderately skilled moderator to go in and read those questions and stick to the script, and then we'll issue some kind of PowerPoint that almost even can be bi banal is going question by question to tell you what people said. That is research I have seen out in the world and I am not a fan. And part of the reason I thought I had enough hubris to think, okay, I can start my own research firm was because I knew there had to be a better way. (00:19:41): And I think a lot of that does come down to that research design process of like, okay, well, let's literally start from that objective. What do we need? Research and marketing land is very different than research and academia. We don't have to do a double blind scientific study on how a placebo affects people versus a heavy dose of a medication that requires complete objectivity, and that requires you to really make sure that your questions are not leading in any way and you're not asking about side effects. Oh, are you having a headache? Because people will try to please a researcher. We know because of all of our internal biases to be agreeable and say yes. So they have to be really careful about the way they phrase questions, the way that they conduct themselves. We're in research in the marketing realm, people. This is just the, I call it the beaker approach compared to the bunsen approach. (00:20:40): If you're a Muppets fan that beaker is just kind of a hot mess and half the time he is lighting the lab on fire, that kind of trial and error and just really trying to go for it is really important in research design and marketing. I think that list of questions, when I look at it a lot of times I'm thinking, how do I make this more pointed? How do I make it more focused? How do I make it more interesting for the respondent? How do I turn this into an activity that they can actually apply some emotive skill sets to not just the sort of frontal lobe of our brain that's going to be very rational when you ask it a rational question, I want someone to play. I want to unleash some playful elements, just like that deprivation study we talked about as one example, right? (00:21:27): Of that's actually not a circumstance many people had encountered in the world. It was an artificial circumstance created to get the result that you needed. You couldn't just list the questions out and call it a day. So that kind of creativity in research design is really what the insight in really strives to do. And that comes from starting, yeah, you might start at that starting point of what do we want to know? But then you've got to think, how can I get there in a way that's really going to create a really evocative experience? Because a lot of times as marketers we're focused on emotion as a strategist in the ad business, I knew that kind of as we talked about earlier, insight was going to be what really mattered to me. So you could come to me and say, oh, I did a bagel study and I came up with seven different reasons that people use bagels. (00:22:18): And 86% of people said that bagels are filling. And this may or may not be an actual example that I've seen a client bring to me as research of 86% of people say bagels are filling. And as sort of the strategist at that time working on marketing and advertising was thinking, well, how do we turn that into something interesting? Where is the material that's going to really scratch under the surface and get people really excited about bagels? And it wasn't going to come from that piece of research. And I kept thinking like, wow, I would've taken that money that they spent on figuring out 86% of people thought bagels were filling and done more of an emotional deep dive into, I don't know what's different about eating a bagel versus eating this. Can you project and tell me how it would feel before, during, and after you are eating a bagel, right? (00:23:07): That's a very different question. That's going to get you a very different outcome. And if people start describing, well, before I eat the bagel, I woke up in the morning, well, how are you feeling? Right? You can really get to some more emotional stuff, but you have to ask the question that way. You can't just sort of go like, okay, well list out the three steps of three different attributes you associate with bagels because filling as number one and number two might be something else, but either one of those might be very inconsequential for marketing purposes. The end result needs to be interesting. So what we're doing with consumers needs to be interesting, and that research design needs to be interesting. And sometimes those projective techniques, as we call them, sometimes they work and sometimes they don't. And that's where the creativity and the being beaker and being willing to blow up the lab comes in of like, wow, this personification exercise where I ask people to personify a bagel as if it was a celebrity that is really not working, people are not able to do that, so let's move on. (00:24:08): Let's throw it in the bin and let's start off down a different path. So I think being really heavy on projective techniques being very heavy and more creative ways of asking questions and being really heavy into emotion is what makes a particularly fascinating way of doing marketing, research, design. And of course, none of those things would apply if you were doing academic or the bunston version of research where you had to be much more buttoned up. You can't do that kind of thing, but we can play an experiment and have fun with it and really try to get to that more interesting result.", "token_estimate": 1590, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:18:27", "end_timestamp": "00:18:47", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "49c6ff82-ccaf-45ee-972a-3a6951369a8d", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:24:44): Yeah. Wow. This research, how is it applicable to marketers? Are there any case studies or projects that you've worked on that you could share some of the insights that you've learned and then some of the decisions that were then made from that insight?\n\nMegan Averell (00:25:01): Yeah. I mean, ultimately every single client has different objectives of what they're trying to get out of something. But a lot of times those questions will come from data. So they'll be like, okay, I can see something in my data that's not quite adding up and I don't understand what why is behind that? What's the consumer motivation behind that? So I had a client that came and said, look, our stuff is getting bought at much less of a rate than we think it should be. People aren't converting nearly as much as they should. And what we don't know, is it the page layout? Is it that they're having trouble understanding from an e-comm perspective how to go about things? Is that functional experience broken? Is the product and the way we're displaying the product on this e-commerce website not appealing? Are there questions we're not answering for them about the way that it's made or the way that people, so you can sit and generate that entire exhaustive list of hypothesis, but essentially your problem is you're not converting people at the rate that you should. (00:26:05): And for this particular client, which was an online men's dress apparel company, they had that extra challenge of you couldn't touch and feel the product. And if there are businessmen listening to this podcast, then that touching and feeling a dress shirt is a big part of how you might buy that dress shirt. So how do you do that and convey all of that and get people comfortable with what you're offering in just in e-comm kind of purchase? Well, that became the heart of the question. So actually what we did is I recruited people, this goes back to research design. We recruited people that were in their target market, really high powered sort of male executives who had to dress a certain way for work. So they were looking at men who were wearing dress wear. And then what we did is I talked to them on an online forum where we just kind of talked upfront about what were they looking for in general when they were looking for, this is all the open-ended stuff. (00:27:01): This is what I call the poll, where you're basically being very open-ended, your hands are completely in the air, and you're just like, tell me how it is to do this. How is it to shop for? What are you looking for? What do you have to feel the men's dress wear? Talk to me about that. And then got all their open-ended feedback. What are some of the great brands that you use? Why do you use those brands? That kind of thing. Then I mailed them product. So we actually took the product and mailed it to them, and during that time, we mailed it to them. The second stage of the research, while this was all happening in behind the scenes was to get them to go to the website. Okay, now site unseen, they were all people that specifically had not heard of this retailer. (00:27:44): That was a key. I wanted them to be very fresh. They came to the website, here's my series of questions about the website up to and including some functional things like put a product in your cart and almost walk through all the way you're going to transact to make sure it wasn't transaction problems. We were kind of checking things off the list, talk to me about the product, how does it feel looking at it, how does it seem to you? That kind of thing. And then of course, meantime, the product was on the way. And the third phase of the research, I actually had them turn on their computer camera and to actually open up the item in front of me to touch it, to feel it. Then I had them go off camera and put it on and come back and talk to me again. (00:28:27): How did it feel to be wearing it? Oh, well, this is interesting, this, I didn't expect this. So what you're getting is you can tell three stages of a full experience. Now, again, this is an artificial experience. There's no way in heck that anyone is ever going to go this exact way through this process. But in diagnosing just everything from their open-ended feedback, what they were currently using to how did they react to the e-comm site as it was to how was it to actually physically put on these things? And then finding the disconnects and the changes between all three of those things and the important threads through all of them. So what were the uniting threads that were really important to them, that all the boxes were checked all the way across the way, and then where were the breakdowns of things that were important to them and they weren't getting across? (00:29:12): And then on the negative side, what wasn't working for them about the website, but actually worked for them once they had the product in hand and looking for those disjoints and disconnects as well, what were they phrasing the way that they were the accurate way, or was it a way that consumers were interpreting it that was very different than the way they were presenting themselves on their website? So that's an example of just taking a different approach in research design and pulling it through to something that was really comprehensive. We were kind of in and out of field within two to three weeks with them, with all of all those pieces working and got them to a really comprehensive conclusion on exactly where that fall off was happening in their purchase consideration, why that was so low.", "token_estimate": 1404, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:24:44", "end_timestamp": "00:25:01", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "4f141699-474a-4d04-b2bb-659f7f813b73", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:29:57): And so from that, were there suggested solutions or did they take that then to their web development team and they start making updates?\n\nMegan Averell (00:30:06): They did almost instantly. I mean, the nice thing about some companies that are on this was on the smaller sort of startup funded end was they were able to make some of those changes immediately. And they did see Lyft over time. In fact, they kind of changed some of not just the day-to-day stuff, which they were able to change right away, but they did change some value proposition stuff. They did change some messaging that was very specifically things that in the game, I like to call it the game of telephone. The things you think you're communicating are often not the thing you're communicating because we use words and phrases for things, and I think any marketer knows that we run into this all the time. Then consumers interpret that. And sometimes that interpretation can go out into left field. It can be like, oh, well you said it's really smooth. (00:30:52): So I think what you mean is that the product is smooth. And it's like, no, I was saying the experience was smooth. You guys missed it. Right? How did you miss that? So what they had found was the way that they were stating their benefit and their unique benefit just wasn't coming across. People just weren't understanding the way that they had phrased it. And so oftentimes it's not like, well, we actually have to overhaul the value proposition. A lot of times it's like, oh, we thought we were getting something across, but when we actually checked in with people, they weren't interpreting it the same way. So if we just tweak the language, they'll be able to come along with us.", "token_estimate": 437, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:29:57", "end_timestamp": "00:30:06", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "90bace78-433c-407b-a481-71b2ee53ffbf", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:31:26): So I feel like that's a great example where I think sometimes we think a lot about on the execution side of we need to update our value proposition, or we need to increase the speed of this website loading in order to increase conversions, but how do we know that those solutions are actually solutions to the problems that we're facing? And this is where it sounds like your job, this is why it exists, because you can come up with, find those problems and then find the solutions, which I got to assume, I think it just goes to show how important good research is. Because if you're going to overhaul the website, that's hundreds of thousands of dollars and you want to make sure that you're redesigning the right parts and making the right changes in order to increase whatever the problem is.", "token_estimate": 206, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:31:26", "end_timestamp": "00:31:26", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "a089cd5e-14b3-4624-acfc-7008592bb2b2", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Megan Averell (00:32:14): Exactly. And there's no lack of hypothesis in a good marketing organization. I mean, that means you're going to sit there and your web team's going to be like, well, when we were designing this, we thought it would be a problem, and now it might be part of the problem. We had a hypothesis that it might be, but then your marketing team is going to be like, actually, I think we might be kind of not getting across this with the way the products look. I mean, some of it just came down to really simple things like they couldn't zoom when they wanted to, so they wanted to click on the product image, and they just wanted to be able to zoom to look at the fabric because of course, if I can't touch the fabric, their nearest best guess was, well, if I can zoom and look at the fabric and the threads and the way they come together, maybe that will help me determine the quality or ascertain the quality. (00:32:58): So it was kind of erroneous. And I think sometimes consumer perception is that, and it kind of sucks because as marketers, we want to live in a place where consumers understand what we understand because that just makes it that much more logical, and it makes it so much more easy to deal with them. But a lot of times they will come up with things like that that you're like, well, of course you can't ascertain by looking at it whether or not the thread count. You're like not an expert. How are your eyeballs really going to tell if this is a quality fabric just by zooming on an image, a static image? That's probably not real, but that is exactly what every single one of these people who'd never met each other and were in private conversation with me one-on-one asked for, right? So there was no influencing of a group. (00:33:45): They were just in private one-on-one, and they were saying, Ooh, I'm having a real frustration where I can't click to zoom to really see it because if I can't touch it, this is basically their proxy now. This is my way of being able to touch it. And that was actually something nobody in the company had thought of. So there was a bunch of hypothesis. Some of them proved to be true about just little problems and issues and things like that. But then there were things like this that consumers just brought out a left field that, okay, well they can't touch it. And the marketers were like, yeah, that's a problem. They can't touch it. But their way of solving that problem was just a little bit off kilter and probably a little different than we would've thought because no one is a site expert on how something's going to feel when you put it on. That's all a little backwards as far as actual logic, but it was their logic. And so a lot of times you have to adjust your framing to that consumer perceptual framing even when it's frustrating, even when you know that it might not. And I guess I keep saying it, it might not be real. I mean, it certainly is very real to them, but it might not have as much basis in fact, as I think a nice clean cut, very straightforward thing would imagine.", "token_estimate": 769, "speakers": ["Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:32:14", "end_timestamp": "00:32:14", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "a404d3b7-56f5-4d28-8679-715b48ea158d", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:34:53): So you're talking about this design process in order to, you have to design a process in order to reach these conclusions and find these insights. And it sounds like from your personal experience, you've come across a bunch of different ways to do this types of research, and it sounds like there's some good practices and there's also probably some practices that lead to, I don't know, not as good as results or not, I guess how would you describe that?", "token_estimate": 121, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:34:53", "end_timestamp": "00:34:53", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "953e54ea-9dc8-4d57-9b9a-c8a49d9baa7a", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Megan Averell (00:35:18): Yeah, I mean, it's funny because I think if you were a really good researcher, you're actually coming up with stuff on the fly all the time, either in session or in your research design process. It's not like, well, now I have my toolkit and my bag of tools as a researcher involves these things. Yes, it does. It certainly does. And I can go back to that toolkit. But if there's not a new and unique situation that you're facing that's not forcing you to build a new tool, I think that's when I worry that I'm just becoming one of those, oh, well, here's the plug and play template. This is the way we deal with conversion issues. It's like, no, that was the way we dealt with conversion issues for that specific retailer's specific problems. So I think a lot of times what I see and when I see research done badly is when it is done by template is when it's like, well, we've had this problem about packaging design before, so let's just drag out the same old materials that we used the last time we exposed packaging and asked people to react. (00:36:17): Let's change the name of the brand from X to Y, and then we'll just go with it and we'll do the same methodology. And it's like, well, yeah, but that packaging may be sitting in a very different store, a very different retailer. It may have very different competitors that have very different packaging, and they may have packaging requirements for how this is going to function. It may have to be a very functional packaging. Well, that's different than just an aesthetic package that you want to look beautiful. So why would we take those same questions even as a starting point? Maybe we're doing ourselves a disservice. So I mean, for me, I think that research design and staying curious and staying open to, well, what if we asked it that way is a huge part of the battle. And so I don't necessarily think, certainly, I think there's an old phrase of the research that's just the bottom drawer research, which is just the research that ends up in the bottom drawer of somebody's desk because it was nice to have, and maybe you learned a tidbit or two, but it's so forgettable and it's so not useful. (00:37:22): It is the 86% of people find bagels are filling, and you go like, okay, and then it just sort of works its way to the bottom of your desk. That is what I am trying to avoid all the time is the kind of research that lands in that drawer and is drawer fodder and not the kind of research that people go back and back and back to. My biggest compliment is when clients come back and say, it's been a year since I have done my research with you, but I have it tucked in my binder that I take to every meeting because there's always something new there for me to find, right? And even though it was a very specific challenge when we asked these specific questions, part of this the way consumers think and feel and are motivated is evergreen as opposed to 86% of bagels are filling is not so evergreen. So I think the fact that these are living documents that help you understand your consumer, and when you have a question about your consumer, if you can come back to that living document, that's a real victory. I think that shows that that research was useful versus not. So what am I trying to do? I'm trying to make research really useful. I have seen, and I think unfortunately too much of it is really drawer fodder not that useful.", "token_estimate": 859, "speakers": ["Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:35:18", "end_timestamp": "00:35:18", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "64374e4d-8072-4333-827b-a45b15e47afc", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:38:34): So if folks are interested, I recommend you go check out Megan's LinkedIn because she's written a number of articles and posts about research fallacies, and we're going to get into a couple of 'em here today, but if we don't cover enough in today's conversation, go check out her LinkedIn. Because you talk a lot about different processes or methods to research that the process itself is ultimately flawed or it's done a poor way, then that gives you poor results. Could you give us your top two or three fallacies that you point to?", "token_estimate": 140, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:38:34", "end_timestamp": "00:38:34", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "de42a734-f7ea-4aeb-a91f-cd4924ed2024", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Megan Averell (00:39:08): Yeah, and I mean there's so many and this and that list is growing every day. There's just little things about research design or process like you mentioned, that can just trip you up and get in the way of findings. Sometimes it's in the findings, in the collection of findings, but even sometimes in the way an organization onboards findings, there's all sorts of places where things can go off into left field or go off the rails and sort of leave that path where we're going to have a really good result. So one of them I had like to talk about is I think when people think of qualitative research, which is the majority of what I do, that they think of focus groups, and when they think of focus groups, their very next thought is focus group facilities. And that is just a terrible way to spend your research dollars, not just that. (00:39:56): It's a bummer of a place to be because it is, if you guys have ever done qualitative research and focus group facilities, they're just not places anybody wants to be. And I'm a big believer in the fact that we take our cues from environment and what is around us and atmosphere. And as marketers, we very much believe in that, or else we wouldn't put so much into our customer experiences and things like that. So why would we put people in sort of an overgrown dentist's office with a two-way mirror and assume that they're going to be natural and emotional and share of their selves in this environment that's incredibly stilted and muted. So that's just one thing with focus groups that I don't love. I could talk about the budget thing, but I see you nodding. I feel like you've had this experience.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:40:40): I have not had this experience, but I'm curious if you can elaborate on what a focus group facility looks like and why. I mean, you mentioned a two-way mirror. This sounds like an interrogation room.", "token_estimate": 477, "speakers": ["Megan Averell", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:39:08", "end_timestamp": "00:40:40", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "30cd217c-8578-479a-81dc-c9a199fac57f", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Megan Averell (00:40:49): It is, right? So that's essentially what it is. And look, I mean there's a time and place for these, but for a long time it was like, okay, we put the consumers in basically what looks like a boardroom except much less nice and much less comfortable because these facilities are cheap. So they're trying to put in the most uncomfortable chairs possible. But here is just a regular conference table. There's usually a seat at the top of that table that the moderator sits in, and then the moderator's back gets to the two-way mirror, which means the consumers have to face toward the two-way mirror, but means they're looking in the mirror the whole time too. I mean, can you imagine running a focus group or being in a focus group and being asked to share your opinions and you're facing sort of a mirror image of yourself? (00:41:29): I think we can now a little bit with zoom, understand that sometimes that throws people off in and of itself. Sometimes that's not natural. It's not how you would normally have a conversation with a fellow human being unless you were a total narcissist. And also it is a situation where your environment really impacts your behavior. And so you're not going to feel like if you're at a bar and you're just chatting up a friend, you're going to reveal some stuff that you might not reveal sitting in a room that basically feels like a corporate office building. And very often they are. So speaking of other queues, we're actually asking people to go to a high rise building a lot of times or a corporate office park. They're parking, paying some exorbitant amount from parking in a parking lot, they're riding up some elevator. And like I said, it kind of ends up looking like a really beige, like a doctor's office, like a waiting room. (00:42:23): And it's like, well, that brings up a whole lot of feelings that are no good because it's the same furniture provider. I swear that's doing all of these spaces. And then we're asking people to give us their best. We're asking people to be open and comfortable and smart and receptive and share of things that we didn't necessarily even ask them about to really be that willing to open themselves. And that's not an environment where anyone feels like that. If anything else, you can kind of feel your s stink or clenching right now. It's like a doctor's office. No, stay away from me. I don't want to talk to you. So that environment is a real bummer. And so if hopefully that paints a picture, but if you all want to Google, you really haven't been inside a focus group facility. They are awful. And they have tried in recent years to update them, which is hilarious to me. (00:43:13): So now they'll have an exposed brick wall and then a two-way mirror and the same table that they've always had. And then sometimes, and this gets into the misappropriation of the budget that I talk about in my post on it, is that they'll actually be like, you can have a couch so that people can feel very natural. So we'll put a couch in the room, we're going to charge you $300 to put that couch in the room. And you're like, okay, this is a really crappy couch you got from Ikea that sits in your storage just down the hall and someone has to pick it up and walk it into this room and you're going to charge me $300 for that, and I'm not entirely sure why. Right? If you're hoping that that makes people slightly more engaged or more comfortable, they can pull their feet up. (00:43:57): They can actually kind of sit in a comfortable posture. Sometimes that's worth it to me as a researcher if I have to do it. But there's still that two-way mirror. There's still all of those uncomfortable waiting room features where they'll actually give them a clipboard a lot of times when they, oh, it drives me nuts. I could go on and on. But the bulk of this, and I think what we of don't think about, because we do oftentimes, oh no, it's another focus group facility. Nobody, not even the clients enjoy them. But there is also the money part that I just touched on where these places kind of make the majority of their money through really incidental details of what you do with your time there. If your client opens a bottle of seltzer, you'll get charged for that the way you do from a mini bar. (00:44:40): But in order to win the bid and look competitive, they basically just do these all as add-on costs. But what they do is they reduce the cost for recruitment and the cost for incentives. Recruitment, you're going to hear me use these words a bit. Recruitment is how much money and time it takes me to find the people I need to talk to. Incentives are what we pay them to show up and talk to this. And both of those things are really important. And I have found in my years of doing this that those actually really contribute more to quality of research than any other variable. How long am I going to spend finding someone is really about time, and that means I need to pay someone enough that they really spend time with that person talking to that person. Are they going to be a really good receptive, open quality respondent or are they just okay? (00:45:31): Do they just check the boxes? Well, if I don't get paid for my time enough, I'm going to take someone who checks all the boxes and go, there you go. But if I'm somebody who is really engaged and finding a great respondent and you've given them by paying them their value, the time to actually interview that person in a quality manner and get to the weak, not just the chaff, then your group, the person that you're bringing in to talk to you is going to be that much better. Then incentives. I think we need to value people. I think we need to value their time. This comes down to an old research method of just like, let's drive vendors on budget. I understand that that's a big budget line item, and it's very easy as a marketer to say, well, why are we spending so much money paying these people? (00:46:14): Shouldn't they be happy with a hundred bucks or 125 bucks for an hour? And it's like, well, that depends. It depends on who you're talking to. If you want to talk to someone who's a pretty busy professional, who's an dual income household and has kids and works at a professional job, then that's probably not a hundred bucks isn't enough for them to drive across town park in that expensive parking lot and come up and talk to you for an hour about all their thoughts and opinions, when actually they could make more money on their own just spending that same time doing their work task. Or they could enjoy themselves more than that a hundred dollars spending time with their family. So you have to make it worth it. And in order to do that, you actually have to overpay people. I'm a big believer in that. (00:46:58): So whereas I think focus group facilities take all their budget out and say, look how cheap I am. They're taking the budget out of what matters, the recruitment and the incentive, and then they're just tacking on so many additional charges from the time that you get there that your bill is actually very hefty by the time you leave. I think it's a total racket, and I wish that that practice would go out of form. So I did have some suggestions in these research fallacy Fridays, I try not to just do and dump on everything and then leave without offering a solution. So my solution for that one was look at community spaces, coffee shops, libraries, even if you have to something like a hotel ballroom or something, there's places that we can meet people that are more comfortable and don't nickel and dime the way that they do. (00:47:47): And then you take that same money you would've spent and you put that into hiring a better recruiter and getting more money to your respondents. Because I have seen it where if I get really good respondents, even just offering them an additional little bit of money, well actually make them open up more because it shows that you value their time so you can have the exact same person. It doesn't have to be a different person. You could have Mike, if Mike is getting paid $125 to be there, Mike's going to sit back in his chair a little bit and be like, alright, I'm going to do as much as I need to get through this and get my money so I could go. But if you pay Mike $250 for his time, he is like, oh crap, you're paying me two $50 for an hour. (00:48:28): I better really contribute. I've got to sit forward. I got to offer an opinion and I have to make this count. I mean, that's all subjective and subconscious. It happens several layers deep. It's not like people are actually going through this level of conscious processing, but a lot of times you can see the difference in their behavior. So after doing a little experimenting over the years myself, I have seen that when clients drive me down on that kind of thing, you can have that same respondent later in a different study two years down the line, but they're suddenly all that much more open and all that much more receptive.", "token_estimate": 2259, "speakers": ["Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:40:49", "end_timestamp": "00:40:49", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "18d1f793-669b-4fb7-ae34-4e6d0d9d1908", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:49:02): So these research facility groups, if I was trying to do research, I would hire one of these facilities and then I would kind of choose based on a menu of what I want the experience to look like, and do they provide the interviewer or are those things I get to choose?\n\nMegan Averell (00:49:20): They sometimes have relationships. So sometimes the reason that clients can use them and look, I get it, marketers are busy, even just to chase them up on getting research details finalized can be difficult. They're going and getting pulled in so many different directions that a lot of times they don't have time. They know what they need to know, but they don't have time to manage every detail. So a lot of times these facilities, I think market themselves as a one-stop shop. Yeah, come here and we can do your recruitment. We can do your incentives and we'll host your group. And sometimes, yes, they do actually have relationships with local moderators in the area who I don't think good all the time, but are folks that are there and available to take on a group. So if you were super busy, I could see that you would see value in that. (00:50:03): But I think your work product that you get out of it is going to be not even 10% of what you would just by going with someone who's independent and on their own and has these tried and true mechanisms in place and really thinks about research design, you could take that exact same amount of money and 10 x it as far as the return. And so that kind of thinking where it's like, oh my God, I understand it's so hard to think about all the different variables. And a lot of times clients are just like, I'm a marketer. I've got too much going on, do it all for me. And that's I think a lot of times where they thrive because they don't ask a lot of questions, not very curious. They're just like, give me the specs of who you need to find, and that's all you need to do. (00:50:44): And there's something attractive about that. I do understand why they persist, but I have worked in my own business now for seven years and I have done three projects inside a facility in that time only. And those were by absolute force where I kind of talked to the client about alternatives, offered them alternatives, and they've said no, but I've even gotten pharmaceutical executives and cellular executives to sit in coffee shop community rooms, right, with consumers right in the same room and listen to them. I think the other thing the two-way mirror does is that creates a bit of separation, doesn't it? I mean it quite physically makes the people in their zoo animals and the people on the back of the room, the smart people who hired the zoo animals to tell them what they wanted. And that sets up just, and again, this is all subconscious processing. (00:51:34): I don't think anyone actually thinks that, but it does set up a certain dynamic, doesn't it? And I'm always trying to be very careful about what those human dynamics are. Whereas if you're sitting in the room with someone, you have to listen to them. You can't be texting your boyfriend or your girlfriend or texting jokes to your colleagues about what that respondent is wearing, that sitting over there. You have to be fully engaged. And so the fact that I've gotten some who I would consider very traditional companies to get out of those traditional venues, I think speaks to the fact that it's actually, it's not too hard to persuade them you're spending your money in the wrong way and actually here's a way to get good. And they've all said, this is my first time doing this. When the very traditional companies do research in a non-traditional way, they'll say, wow, I got a lot out of this. I got a lot out of being there. First. I was scared about being in the room with people, but it actually worked out just fine. It's like, yeah, we can all just be humans. We can just be human beings having conversations.", "token_estimate": 989, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:49:02", "end_timestamp": "00:49:20", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "cb375273-992f-47f9-9c4c-7f0c953786e3", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:52:33): Yeah. Interesting. So what, what's fallacy number two?\n\nMegan Averell (00:52:39): Alright, so I think another one is that people overstuff their guides. So I think this is a sign in some ways that research isn't done very often at all. So when research is done, everyone in the organization, from operations to customer support to hr, to anyone who works in a different division might come to marketing and say, oh, hey, yeah, we had these questions. Also, could you just put those in? And a lot of times marketing is like, ah, well we haven't done research in a while, so could we get as many of these questions as humanly possible? I actually just worked with a client last week and they sent me a 10 page discussion guide for a 45 minute focus group, and that was single spaced. And I said to them, I counted everything up. And I said, okay, this is your wishlist of questions. (00:53:25): If I put everything in that we had here, that would equate to about 90 seconds per question. And that's not enough in qualitative because it's going to take me 20 seconds to get the question out of my mouth. So now we're down to a minute, they have a minute to answer, and then I've got to move them to the next. I'm sorry, I have to move you on. I'm sorry, I have to move you on. That is an auctioneer that is not a moderator. And especially in qualitative research, you have to give things space to breathe because people are not always aware of what motivates them. If I asked you why you bought your favorite toothpaste, you're probably going to have some first BLS reactions. Oh, well, it's something I've always used. It's something maybe my family used. I dunno. It's kind of got this flavor that I like and I didn't really, I tried this other flavor, but I didn't like it. (00:54:13): I like this. Cool, but that's not really why you're using it. That's just some of the what, so I need to sit with that and I need to help you sit with that. And because toothpaste is a low involvement choice that people don't think a lot about, I'm probably going to have to use some projective techniques now. We're probably going to have to get into like, well, what is it about this? What does this brand say to you? Let's use a projective technique to help us find that. Oh, it says I'm a smart shopper. Arm and hammer or whatever might be about cleanliness. And cleanliness is interesting to me as it relates to my toothpaste. Those are the things that take a little bit of time. So if I'm just saying, what is it about your toothpaste? And then I have to move on to something else, I'm not really getting much other than your first plus reaction. (00:54:53): And if I wanted your hot take, I would just go to the internet. There's plenty of hot takes about products and services on there that aren't very well thought through. So I could just scan people's social media comments or the people who are on the reviews of the products who've barely used it, and then they put like, oh, I've just started using this button. And you're like, well, this isn't really helpful. To me, that is the equivalent of the depth we're at when we ask that question off the bat, people are getting that first blush reaction. And so we need time. And oftentimes my favorite research studies are when clients come and they have two or three key questions in 45 minutes or an hour, because then I can really get in there and answer them as opposed to, I have to talk to these people that work in this job. (00:55:39): And I start out by asking them, what do you like about your job? What are the upsides? What are the downsides? Yeah, we're kind of easing them in, we're getting them into the conversation. No, we're actually taking time away from what you really want to know, which is at the back of this thing. So if we actually kind of just even look at the way we're going to talk about things, why do we have this assumption that we have to spend 10 or 15 minutes warming people up to a topic? Do we? They're professionals. They do this job for a living. We know what it is that they do, and unless this question is germane to the actual output we need, we're just wasting time. So how do we really key in on what those two or three or four topics really are that we really want to know? (00:56:19): And then how do we go deep? That's what qualitative is for. If you want the stuff, go do quant, which is fine. Don't get me wrong. There's a time and place for knowing, make this list of attributes that matter to you when you're buying toothpaste. But that's a quant study and that's easy to go find that way. But if I really want to unpack all of that, I need time. So I think the other thing, it just, and in my world, the way I spun that one out at the end as I always try to offer some nice solution. And for that one, it's okay, can we offer researchers just less? Can we offer them fewer objectives that are more buttoned down? But also I think it's maybe we need to do research more often. Cole, you mentioned upfront. Wow, it seems like this gets to a lot of the why of questions we have a lot of hypothesis about, but can't be sure of. (00:57:06): And it's like, yes, if we were doing it more regularly, I mean, I've worked with firms in the outdoor space and things like that, that'll be like, oh, it's been years since we've done research. And I'm thinking, my God, how many business decisions have you made on assumptions since then? So then, yeah, sure. When I get you, you're going to be like, oh, we need to ask about this thing we did three years ago because we have a whole different product line now and we haven't ever asked people about it, so we need to get that in. And you're like, well, that literally doesn't have any relation to what we're talking to people about, but we can fit it in if you need to. That's the kind of situation you don't necessarily want to be in. And the answer for that is an industry plugging one, which is do more research in general so you don't have to overpack yourself.", "token_estimate": 1507, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:52:33", "end_timestamp": "00:52:39", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "0e97db1a-4f97-4f5c-a7af-298571373a4e", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:57:46): Wow. I feel like this entire conversation is just chockfull of really fascinating pieces of information that could probably go on for another hour. But Megan, I'm curious to learn a little bit more about why you personally gravitate towards this. Are you a naturally curious person? Do you like to interview people? What about the field of research makes you want to be here?\n\nMegan Averell (00:58:09): Yeah, I think I often hear from folks in the industry that are, other researchers are like, yeah, I'm curious about people. But I also feel like I hear that from marketers a lot. I mean, I don't think people got into marketing so that they could push numbers around an Excel spreadsheet all day long. There is a fundamental truth I think that unites marketing and research, which is we kind of want to know what's driving people, what's up with that? We see things and our first reaction is what's up with that human behavior? It's the same thing that we get from psychologists and things like that too, but in that much more clinical sort of setting. So I think we share that. I don't think that's unique to research. I don't, but I think the intensity of that can be unique to research. I think you really do have to find someone, and if I was looking for a researcher personally, I'd be looking for someone who nerds out hardcore on those elements. It doesn't just say those nice things of like, yes, I really care what people think, but is committed to finding out and is committed to trying different things to get there. (00:59:17): I think a way I just woke up realized, oh my gosh, I want to be a researcher. But I do think the one thing that continually frustrated me that led me into research was that in marketing, we do sit at our desks for most of our day thinking about what people think, but we are not necessarily able to do that with knowledge of actual straight firsthand knowledge of what people think. And that's frustrating is like I've written a million personas in my life. I've written a bazillion customer journeys in my life, and I've written even more value propositions and things like that that have briefs that have a consumer blank in them. But the times I was able to talk to consumers in that 10 years were so fractional that at some point I realized this is made up and we are firmly in fantasy land and I can be very good at what I do and still be very wrong. (01:00:10): And I felt like that little dose of really well done research for me could have been the difference maker over that time and to getting from stuff that I thought was pointing in the right direction to getting to stuff that I knew was pointed in the right direction and was actually going to compel people. I mean, we are in sort of that business of resonance and being compelling. If you are saying stuff, it doesn't matter how factual it is. It doesn't matter if you're 20% better than your competitor. What matters is, is it resonant with them personally? Does it compel them to act? And of course, what we know and what I know as a researcher is that apathy is our biggest motivation. That's our biggest motivation. And so you're having to break through people's biggest motivation in order to get to those next level sets of motivations if you think about it, to get them to change behavior, to try your product or service. (01:01:04): So for me, it was the need I felt a need and a compelling need to know versus just making those assumptions. And now I still do freelance strategy work. I still do consultancy work because I want to keep that saw really sharp, and I want to continue to be someone who does research that can be implemented when I'm over on that side. I feel much better at my job now. I feel much better at the strategy role because I do know where consumers are at almost all the time. And that's a very different feeling than just being like, wow, I got to go to those focus groups one time a year. Or my client actually approved primary research where we got to go out and talk to people in one-on-ones one time a year ago, which is, it was just a glacial pace I felt like for talking to consumers and I couldn't live with that. There was just part of me, it was just like, I just need to bring, even if it's two focus groups, even if it's a minuscule amount of direction, I need to bring some direction to those assumptions, which they very often can be incredibly well, well-formed assumptions really well thought through. But there's still assumptions.", "token_estimate": 1130, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "00:57:46", "end_timestamp": "00:58:09", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "5e471d87-31ef-457d-bc80-2a594bdf1fd7", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:02:12): Is there anything that we didn't cover today that you want to include? Anything that you want to make note of?\n\nMegan Averell (01:02:19): I think it's just a reminder, and I think a lot of my fallacy posts tend to be about the fact of consumers just thinking so differently than we do. So I think if I could leave everyone on the podcast with anything, it's just that we have these again, really well thought out hypothesis, and a lot of times consumers are basing their opinions on your brands about the most minuscule things you've never thought of. So in one of my articles, I talk about how consumers are often building their brand perceptions from the ground up, and we're building them from the top down. We're going, here's our premise, and then here's everything that falls neatly into beautiful boxes under that premise or pillars or whatever we want to call them. And meanwhile, consumers are just having tangible experiences and so much of what they believe about you actually can ladder quite highly based on a tangible experience. (01:03:09): So for example, consumers have told me that they listen to the ways employees interact with each other when they're on the job. So if I'm a consumer and I'm sitting in a waiting room, or I'm sitting at a checkout stand and I'm watching the way employees treat each other, treat one another, I'm forming impressions about the brand and the way that the brand either trains or treats its people. By the way, my employees are just having what they think might be a private exchange or conversation. And that can be indicative to them of like, well, I bet they're paying them really good wages. They seem really happy, they seem to really love their jobs. And it's like, well, that again, may not be true, but these are examples of the laddering mechanisms. Consumers build from really small, tangible experiences that a lot of times we do not think about. (01:03:55): But companies that do, there's oil change companies where if you go to the fast oil change, I don't know if you've had this experience where you hear them communicating with each other in a really crisp manner, right? Check three, check five, and then they're kind of working in tandem and as a team in a really tight manner. I've had consumers tell me that that feels phenomenal to them, and it's not technically a consumer touchpoint and something that's happening around them, but it's something their senses are observing, and then they're making some sometimes very flawed assumptions about companies and brand based on those. So I think if I could leave you with anything, and the reason I'm leaving you with that is because I do think it underscores the power of research, and especially qualitative research. If I'm in the car with that person going through that instant oil change and I'm physically sitting with them as a researcher and I'm able to hear them react in the moment to these things, then that could tell you that the way your employees are communicating with each other might be as important, if not more important than that thing you wrote on that beautiful piece of paper that's sitting on the deck, that's sitting in the corporate office that says what your brand is all about. (01:05:01): So I really think there is an inherent value, even for something silly or small or tangible, that can really lead them down the path of steering their entire thinking about who you guys are as an organization. Wow, there is so much power in going and finding those things out and being inexperiences with them. So yeah, that's just an example of how different their world is and how different their framing is on all of this. And I know we inherently know that. I know that we inherently get that, but when time is short and budget is short, it's just so much easier to go, okay, well, we've got that. We've got our documents in place, we know what we're doing. But those touch points, I have never, ever failed to bring a client something they didn't know. And that's the magic of it, is we all have these hypothesis, but I don't want to scare people when I tell them, it's never what you think. There's always something else going on there. We are just so flawed in our thinking as creatures and so messed up in our thinking that it's always something you never could have imagined because those mind frames are just night and day.", "token_estimate": 1099, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "01:02:12", "end_timestamp": "01:02:19", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}]
